{"probe_id":"code.layout_composition.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.layout_composition","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"layout_kind":"nested_mixed"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Produce CSS rules only. Implement a magazine layout in which `.page` is a CSS grid with `grid-template-areas: 'hero hero' 'body aside' 'footer footer'`, `grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr`, and `gap: 24px`. Set `.hero`, `.body`, `.aside`, and `.footer` to `grid-area: hero`, `body`, `aside`, and `footer`, respectively. Inside `.hero`, use a flex row with both main-axis and cross-axis centering. Inside `.body`, use a vertical flex layout with `gap: 16px`.","ground_truth":".page{display:grid;grid-template-areas:'hero hero' 'body aside' 'footer footer';grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;gap:24px;}\n.hero{grid-area:hero;display:flex;flex-direction:row;justify-content:center;align-items:center;}\n.body{grid-area:body;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;}\n.aside{grid-area:aside;}\n.footer{grid-area:footer;}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"source_contract","checks":{"balanced_delimiters":true,"case_sensitive":true,"source_language":"c_like","required_patterns":["\\.page\\s*\\{[^{}]*display\\s*:\\s*grid\\s*;","\\.page\\s*\\{[^{}]*grid-template-columns\\s*:\\s*2fr\\s+1fr\\s*;","\\.page\\s*\\{[^{}]*gap\\s*:\\s*24px\\s*;","\\.hero\\s*\\{[^{}]*grid-area\\s*:\\s*hero\\s*;","\\.hero\\s*\\{[^{}]*display\\s*:\\s*flex\\s*;","\\.hero\\s*\\{[^{}]*justify-content\\s*:\\s*center\\s*;","\\.hero\\s*\\{[^{}]*align-items\\s*:\\s*center\\s*;","\\.body\\s*\\{[^{}]*grid-area\\s*:\\s*body\\s*;","\\.body\\s*\\{[^{}]*display\\s*:\\s*flex\\s*;","\\.body\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex-direction\\s*:\\s*column\\s*;","\\.body\\s*\\{[^{}]*gap\\s*:\\s*16px\\s*;","\\.aside\\s*\\{[^{}]*grid-area\\s*:\\s*aside\\s*;","\\.footer\\s*\\{[^{}]*grid-area\\s*:\\s*footer\\s*;"],"required_any_patterns":[],"forbidden_patterns":["\\.hero\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex-direction\\s*:\\s*(?:column|column-reverse|row-reverse)\\s*;"],"pattern_counts":{"\\{":5,"\\}":5},"required_literal_patterns":["grid-template-areas\\s*:\\s*['\\\"]hero hero['\\\"]\\s*['\\\"]body aside['\\\"]\\s*['\\\"]footer footer['\\\"]\\s*;"]}},"tags":["layout","grid_areas","nested"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.layout_composition.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.layout_composition","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"layout_kind":"nested_mixed"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `Inspector` using inline styles only. It must fill the viewport height with an outer flex column. Render an auto-height header above a body that fills the remaining height and is a flex row. The body must contain, in order: a left tree pane fixed at 200px and vertically scrollable, a center detail pane that takes the remaining width and is vertically scrollable, and a right inspector pane fixed at 280px and vertically scrollable. The fixed side panes must not shrink. Ensure nested scrolling works. Accept `header`, `tree`, `detail`, and `inspector` as ReactNode props. Output only the component.","ground_truth":"import type { ReactNode } from 'react';\n\ntype InspectorProps = {\n  header: ReactNode;\n  tree: ReactNode;\n  detail: ReactNode;\n  inspector: ReactNode;\n};\n\nexport function Inspector({ header, tree, detail, inspector }: InspectorProps) {\n  return (\n    <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', height: '100vh' }}>\n      <div>{header}</div>\n      <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'row', flex: 1, minHeight: 0 }}>\n        <div style={{ flex: '0 0 200px', overflowY: 'auto' }}>{tree}</div>\n        <div style={{ flex: 1, minWidth: 0, overflowY: 'auto' }}>{detail}</div>\n        <div style={{ flex: '0 0 280px', overflowY: 'auto' }}>{inspector}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Inspector"],"calls":[],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["minHeight\\s*:\\s*0","minWidth\\s*:\\s*0"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{"overflowY\\s*:":3},"required_literal_patterns":["height\\s*:\\s*['\\\"]100vh['\\\"]","flexDirection\\s*:\\s*['\\\"]column['\\\"]","flexDirection\\s*:\\s*['\\\"]row['\\\"]"],"required_any_literal_patterns":[["\\bwidth\\s*:\\s*200\\b[\\s\\S]*\\bflexShrink\\s*:\\s*0\\b","\\bflex\\s*:\\s*['\\\"]0\\s+0\\s+200px['\\\"]"],["\\bwidth\\s*:\\s*280\\b[\\s\\S]*\\bflexShrink\\s*:\\s*0\\b","\\bflex\\s*:\\s*['\\\"]0\\s+0\\s+280px['\\\"]"]]}},"tags":["layout","nested","three_pane"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.layout_composition.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.layout_composition","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"layout_kind":"nested_mixed"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Produce CSS rules only. The markup for each column is `.column > .header`, `.column > .cards`, and `.column > .footer`. Create a Trello-like board where `.board` is a horizontal, horizontally scrollable flex row with a definite height of `100vh`; each `.column` is a non-shrinking 280px-wide vertical flex container filling the board height. The header must use `position: sticky` and `top: 0`; `.cards` must be a vertical flex list with an 8px gap, take the remaining available height, and scroll vertically; the footer must not grow or shrink.","ground_truth":".board{display:flex;flex-direction:row;gap:12px;overflow-x:auto;height:100vh;}\n.column{display:flex;flex:0 0 280px;flex-direction:column;height:100%;min-height:0;}\n.column>.header{position:sticky;top:0;background:#fff;z-index:1;}\n.column>.cards{display:flex;flex:1 1 auto;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;min-height:0;overflow-y:auto;}\n.column>.footer{flex:0 0 auto;}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"source_contract","checks":{"balanced_delimiters":true,"case_sensitive":true,"source_language":"c_like","required_patterns":["\\.board\\s*\\{[^{}]*display\\s*:\\s*flex\\s*;","\\.board\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex-direction\\s*:\\s*row\\s*;","\\.board\\s*\\{[^{}]*overflow-x\\s*:\\s*auto\\s*;","\\.board\\s*\\{[^{}]*height\\s*:\\s*100vh\\s*;","\\.column\\s*\\{[^{}]*display\\s*:\\s*flex\\s*;","\\.column\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex\\s*:\\s*0\\s+0\\s+280px\\s*;","\\.column\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex-direction\\s*:\\s*column\\s*;","(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.header\\s*\\{[^{}]*position\\s*:\\s*sticky\\s*;","(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.header\\s*\\{[^{}]*top\\s*:\\s*0\\s*;","(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.cards\\s*\\{[^{}]*display\\s*:\\s*flex\\s*;","(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.cards\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex-direction\\s*:\\s*column\\s*;","(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.cards\\s*\\{[^{}]*gap\\s*:\\s*8px\\s*;","(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.cards\\s*\\{[^{}]*overflow-y\\s*:\\s*auto\\s*;"],"required_any_patterns":[["\\.column\\s*\\{[^{}]*height\\s*:\\s*100%\\s*;","\\.board\\s*\\{[^{}]*align-items\\s*:\\s*stretch\\s*;"],["(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.cards\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex\\s*:\\s*1\\s+1\\s+(?:auto|0)\\s*;"],["(?:\\.column\\s*>\\s*)?\\.footer\\s*\\{[^{}]*flex\\s*:\\s*(?:0\\s+0\\s+auto|none)\\s*;"]],"forbidden_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{"\\{":5,"\\}":5}}},"tags":["layout","sticky","nested"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"return_shape":"scalar"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function with the EXACT signature `def double(x: int) -> int:` that returns 2*x. Output only the function definition.","ground_truth":"def double(x: int) -> int:\n    return 2*x","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"double","tests":["assert double(0) == 0","assert double(3) == 6","assert double(-5) == -10","assert double(1 << 30) == (1 << 31)","import inspect","sig = inspect.signature(double)","assert list(sig.parameters) == ['x']","assert sig.parameters['x'].annotation is int","assert sig.return_annotation is int"]},"tags":["signature","scalar"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"return_shape":"scalar"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function with EXACT signature `def is_even(n: int) -> bool:` returning True iff n is even. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def is_even(n: int) -> bool:\n    return n % 2 == 0","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"is_even","tests":["assert is_even(0) is True","assert is_even(2) is True","assert is_even(3) is False","assert is_even(-4) is True","import inspect","sig = inspect.signature(is_even)","assert list(sig.parameters) == ['n']","param = sig.parameters['n']","assert param.kind is inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD","assert param.annotation is int","assert sig.return_annotation is bool"]},"tags":["signature","scalar","bool"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"return_shape":"scalar"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function with EXACT signature `def greet(name: str) -> str:` returning the string 'Hello, ' followed by name and a period. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def greet(name: str) -> str:\n    return 'Hello, ' + name + '.'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"greet","tests":["assert greet('Ann') == 'Hello, Ann.'","assert greet('') == 'Hello, .'","assert greet('Bob') == 'Hello, Bob.'","assert greet('A B') == 'Hello, A B.'","import inspect; sig = inspect.signature(greet); assert list(sig.parameters) == ['name']; p = sig.parameters['name']; assert p.kind is inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD; assert p.default is inspect.Parameter.empty; assert p.annotation is str; assert sig.return_annotation is str"]},"tags":["signature","string"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L1.004","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"return_shape":"scalar"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function with EXACT signature `def absdiff(a: float, b: float) -> float:` returning |a-b|. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def absdiff(a: float, b: float) -> float:\n    return abs(a - b)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert absdiff(3.0, 1.0) == 2.0","assert absdiff(-1.0, 1.0) == 2.0","assert absdiff(0.0, 0.0) == 0.0","assert absdiff(2.5, 5.5) == 3.0","import math; assert math.isclose(absdiff(0.1, 0.4), 0.3)","import inspect; sig = inspect.signature(absdiff); assert list(sig.parameters) == ['a', 'b']","import inspect; sig = inspect.signature(absdiff); assert all(p.kind is inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD for p in sig.parameters.values())","import inspect; sig = inspect.signature(absdiff); assert all(p.default is inspect.Parameter.empty for p in sig.parameters.values())","import inspect; sig = inspect.signature(absdiff); assert sig.parameters['a'].annotation is float and sig.parameters['b'].annotation is float","import inspect; assert inspect.signature(absdiff).return_annotation is float"]},"tags":["signature","two_args"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L1.005","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"return_shape":"scalar"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function with EXACT signature `def length(s: str) -> int:`. Return the number of Python string characters obtained by iterating over `s`. Do not call `len()` anywhere; count manually with a loop. Output only the function definition.","ground_truth":"def length(s: str) -> int:\n    n = 0\n    for _ in s: n += 1\n    return n","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert length('') == 0","assert length('a') == 1","assert length('abcd') == 4","assert length(' ' * 10) == 10","assert length('hé😊') == 3","import inspect; sig = inspect.signature(length); assert list(sig.parameters) == ['s']; assert sig.parameters['s'].kind is inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD; assert sig.parameters['s'].default is inspect.Parameter.empty; assert sig.parameters['s'].annotation is str; assert sig.return_annotation is int"]},"tags":["signature","scalar","int"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L2.001","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"return_shape":"container"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function with EXACT signature `def squares(n: int) -> list[int]:` returning a list of [0**2, 1**2, ..., (n-1)**2]. Empty list when n<=0. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def squares(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    return [i * i for i in range(max(n, 0))]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["import inspect","sig = inspect.signature(squares)","assert list(sig.parameters) == ['n']","param = sig.parameters['n']","assert param.kind is inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD","assert param.default is inspect.Parameter.empty","assert param.annotation is int","assert sig.return_annotation == list[int]","assert squares(0) == []","assert squares(-3) == []","assert squares(1) == [0]","assert squares(5) == [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]","assert isinstance(squares(3), list)"]},"tags":["signature","list_return"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L2.002","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"return_shape":"container"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python 3.9+ function with EXACT signature `def char_freq(s: str) -> dict[str, int]:` mapping each character of `s` to its count. An empty string must return `{}`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def char_freq(s: str) -> dict[str, int]:\n    out = {}\n    for c in s:\n        out[c] = out.get(c, 0) + 1\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["import inspect; assert str(inspect.signature(char_freq)) == '(s: str) -> dict[str, int]'","assert char_freq('') == {}","assert char_freq('a') == {'a': 1}","assert char_freq('aab') == {'a': 2, 'b': 1}","assert char_freq('abc') == {'a': 1, 'b': 1, 'c': 1}","assert char_freq('a a!') == {'a': 2, ' ': 1, '!': 1}","assert isinstance(char_freq('x'), dict)"]},"tags":["signature","dict_return"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L2.003","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"return_shape":"container"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write exactly one Python function with this signature: `def heap_queue_largest(nums: list[int], n: int) -> list[int]:`. Assume `0 <= n <= len(nums)`. Return the n largest integers from nums in descending order. Output only the function definition, with no explanation.","ground_truth":"def heap_queue_largest(nums: list[int], n: int) -> list[int]:\n    import heapq\n    return heapq.nlargest(n, nums)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["import inspect; sig = inspect.signature(heap_queue_largest); assert list(sig.parameters) == ['nums', 'n']; assert sig.return_annotation == list[int]","assert heap_queue_largest([85,75,65,45,35,25,15], 3) == [85,75,65]","assert heap_queue_largest([1,2,3], 2) == [3,2]","assert heap_queue_largest([], 0) == []","assert heap_queue_largest([5,5,5], 2) == [5,5]","assert heap_queue_largest([1], 1) == [1]","assert heap_queue_largest([3,1,2], 3) == [3,2,1]"]},"tags":["signature","list_return","sorted_desc"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L2.004","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"return_shape":"container"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `partition(xs: list[int], pivot: int) -> dict[str, list[int]]` returning a dict with keys 'lt', 'eq', 'gt' listing elements respectively less than, equal to, greater than pivot, preserving original order within each group. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def partition(xs: list[int], pivot: int) -> dict[str, list[int]]:\n    out = {'lt': [], 'eq': [], 'gt': []}\n    for x in xs:\n        if x < pivot:\n            out['lt'].append(x)\n        elif x == pivot:\n            out['eq'].append(x)\n        else:\n            out['gt'].append(x)\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"partition","tests":["from typing import get_type_hints; assert get_type_hints(partition) == {'xs': list[int], 'pivot': int, 'return': dict[str, list[int]]}","assert partition([], 0) == {'lt': [], 'eq': [], 'gt': []}","assert partition([3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6], 4) == {'lt': [3, 1, 1, 2], 'eq': [4], 'gt': [5, 9, 6]}","assert set(partition([1, 2, 3], 2).keys()) == {'lt', 'eq', 'gt'}","assert partition([5, 5, 5], 5) == {'lt': [], 'eq': [5, 5, 5], 'gt': []}","assert partition([1], 1) == {'lt': [], 'eq': [1], 'gt': []}"]},"tags":["signature","dict_return"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L2.005","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"return_shape":"container"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write exactly one Python function with the signature `def unique_sorted(xs: list[int]) -> list[int]:` that returns the unique values in `xs` in ascending order. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def unique_sorted(xs: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    return sorted(set(xs))","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"unique_sorted","tests":["import inspect","sig = inspect.signature(unique_sorted)","assert list(sig.parameters) == ['xs']","assert sig.parameters['xs'].annotation == list[int]","assert sig.return_annotation == list[int]","assert unique_sorted([]) == []","assert unique_sorted([3,1,2,3,1]) == [1,2,3]","assert unique_sorted([5]) == [5]","assert unique_sorted([-1,-1,0]) == [-1,0]","assert isinstance(unique_sorted([1]), list)"]},"tags":["signature","list_return"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L3.001","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"return_shape":"tuple_of_N"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write only a Python function `divmod_pair(a: int, b: int) -> tuple[int, int]`. Assume `b != 0`. Return a 2-tuple `(quotient, remainder)`, with quotient first and remainder second, exactly matching Python's `divmod(a, b)` semantics (equivalently, `q = a // b` and `r = a - q * b`).","ground_truth":"def divmod_pair(a: int, b: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    q = a // b\n    r = a - q * b\n    return (q, r)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"divmod_pair","tests":["assert divmod_pair(10, 3) == (3, 1)","assert divmod_pair(-7, 2) == (-4, 1)","assert divmod_pair(7, -3) == (-3, -2)","assert divmod_pair(-7, -2) == (3, -1)","assert divmod_pair(0, 5) == (0, 0)","assert divmod_pair(20, 4) == (5, 0)","assert isinstance(divmod_pair(1, 1), tuple) and len(divmod_pair(1, 1)) == 2"]},"tags":["signature","tuple"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L3.002","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"return_shape":"tuple_of_N"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write only a Python function with exactly this signature: `def sum_product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:`. Return a 2-tuple whose first element is the sum and whose second element is the product of all integers in `numbers`. For an empty list, return `(0, 1)`.","ground_truth":"def sum_product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    s = 0\n    p = 1\n    for n in numbers:\n        s += n\n        p *= n\n    return (s, p)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["import inspect","sig = inspect.signature(sum_product)","assert list(sig.parameters) == ['numbers']","assert sig.parameters['numbers'].annotation == list[int]","assert sig.return_annotation == tuple[int, int]","assert sum_product([]) == (0, 1)","assert sum_product([1, 2, 3, 4]) == (10, 24)","assert sum_product([5]) == (5, 5)","assert sum_product([0, 5, 5]) == (10, 0)","assert sum_product([-2, 3]) == (1, -6)","assert isinstance(sum_product([1, 2]), tuple) and len(sum_product([1, 2])) == 2"]},"tags":["signature","tuple_of_2"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"return_shape":"tuple_of_N"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write exactly `def min_mid_max(xs: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int, int]:`. Return a 3-tuple `(minimum, median, maximum)` for the non-empty integer list `xs`. The median is the lower middle element of `sorted(xs)` when the length is even, i.e. `sorted(xs)[(len(xs)-1)//2]`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def min_mid_max(xs: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int, int]:\n    s = sorted(xs)\n    return (s[0], s[(len(s) - 1) // 2], s[-1])","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["import inspect","sig = inspect.signature(min_mid_max)","assert list(sig.parameters) == ['xs']","assert sig.parameters['xs'].annotation == list[int]","assert sig.return_annotation == tuple[int, int, int]","assert min_mid_max([1]) == (1, 1, 1)","assert min_mid_max([3,1,2]) == (1,2,3)","assert min_mid_max([4,1,3,2]) == (1,2,4)","assert min_mid_max([0,-5,-1,-5]) == (-5,-5,0)","assert min_mid_max([5,5,5,5]) == (5,5,5)","assert isinstance(min_mid_max([1,2,3]), tuple) and len(min_mid_max([1,2,3])) == 3"]},"tags":["signature","tuple_of_3"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"return_shape":"tuple_of_N"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `partition_indices(xs: list[int], pivot: int) -> tuple[list[int], list[int], list[int]]` returning a 3-tuple of index lists (lt_indices, eq_indices, gt_indices). Original order preserved within each group. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def partition_indices(xs, pivot):\n    lt, eq, gt = [], [], []\n    for i, x in enumerate(xs):\n        if x < pivot: lt.append(i)\n        elif x == pivot: eq.append(i)\n        else: gt.append(i)\n    return (lt, eq, gt)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"partition_indices","tests":["assert partition_indices([], 0) == ([],[],[])","assert partition_indices([1,2,3,2,1], 2) == ([0,4],[1,3],[2])","r = partition_indices([5,5,5], 5); assert r == ([],[0,1,2],[])","r = partition_indices([3,1,2], 2); assert r == ([1],[2],[0])","assert isinstance(partition_indices([1],1), tuple) and len(partition_indices([1],1)) == 3"]},"tags":["signature","nested_containers"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_compliance.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.signature_compliance","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"return_shape":"tuple_of_N"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `parse_url_parts(url: str) -> tuple[str, str, dict[str, str]]` returning `(scheme, host, query_params_dict)`. Inputs always contain `scheme://host`, may contain a path and an optional query, and contain no fragment. Ignore the path. Query items, when present, are `key=value` pairs joined by `&`; do not URL-decode them. If a key appears more than once, keep its last value. An absent or empty query yields `{}`. Output only the function, including the specified type annotations.","ground_truth":"def parse_url_parts(url: str) -> tuple[str, str, dict[str, str]]:\n    scheme, rest = url.split('://', 1)\n    host_path, sep, query = rest.partition('?')\n    host = host_path.split('/', 1)[0]\n    params: dict[str, str] = {}\n    if sep and query:\n        for item in query.split('&'):\n            key, value = item.split('=', 1)\n            params[key] = value\n    return scheme, host, params","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"parse_url_parts","tests":["import inspect","sig = inspect.signature(parse_url_parts)","assert list(sig.parameters) == ['url']","assert sig.parameters['url'].annotation is str","assert sig.return_annotation == tuple[str, str, dict[str, str]]","assert parse_url_parts('https://a.com') == ('https', 'a.com', {})","assert parse_url_parts('http://x.io/path?a=1&b=2') == ('http', 'x.io', {'a':'1', 'b':'2'})","assert parse_url_parts('ftp://h/?k=v') == ('ftp', 'h', {'k':'v'})","assert parse_url_parts('https://e.com/p') == ('https', 'e.com', {})","assert parse_url_parts('https://e.com?') == ('https', 'e.com', {})","assert parse_url_parts('https://e.com/p?a=1&a=2') == ('https', 'e.com', {'a':'2'})"]},"tags":["signature","nested_containers","tuple"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"easy","algorithm_class":"library_use","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write only a Python function definition with the exact signature `def gcd(a: int, b: int) -> int:`. It must return the greatest common divisor of two non-negative integers, with `gcd(0, 0) == 0`. Do not use input/output operations or print anything.","ground_truth":"def gcd(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["import math","assert gcd(0, 0) == 0","assert gcd(12, 18) == 6","assert gcd(17, 5) == 1","assert gcd(100, 25) == 25","assert gcd(48, 0) == 48","assert gcd(0, 48) == 48","assert gcd(99991, 97) == math.gcd(99991, 97)","for a in range(101):\n    for b in range(101):\n        assert gcd(a, b) == math.gcd(a, b)"]},"tags":["algo_design","easy"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"easy","algorithm_class":"library_use","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool` returning True iff s reads the same forward and backward. Compare characters directly (no normalization). Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:\n    return s == s[::-1]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"is_palindrome","tests":["assert is_palindrome('') == True","assert is_palindrome('a') == True","assert is_palindrome('aba') == True","assert is_palindrome('abc') == False","assert is_palindrome('abba') == True","assert is_palindrome('Aba') == False","assert is_palindrome('a b a') == True","assert is_palindrome('a b') == False","assert is_palindrome('abca') == False"]},"tags":["algo_design","easy"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"easy","algorithm_class":"library_use","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `fizzbuzz(n: int) -> list[str]` for a non-negative integer `n`. Return a list of length `n` whose i-th element, for i from 1 through n, is `'FizzBuzz'` if i is divisible by 15, `'Fizz'` if i is divisible by 3, `'Buzz'` if i is divisible by 5, and `str(i)` otherwise. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def fizzbuzz(n):\n    out = []\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        if i % 15 == 0: out.append('FizzBuzz')\n        elif i % 3 == 0: out.append('Fizz')\n        elif i % 5 == 0: out.append('Buzz')\n        else: out.append(str(i))\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert fizzbuzz(0) == []","assert fizzbuzz(1) == ['1']","assert fizzbuzz(5) == ['1', '2', 'Fizz', '4', 'Buzz']","assert fizzbuzz(15) == ['1', '2', 'Fizz', '4', 'Buzz', 'Fizz', '7', '8', 'Fizz', 'Buzz', '11', 'Fizz', '13', '14', 'FizzBuzz']","assert fizzbuzz(16)[-2:] == ['FizzBuzz', '16']","assert fizzbuzz(30)[-1] == 'FizzBuzz'","assert len(fizzbuzz(100)) == 100 and fizzbuzz(100)[-1] == 'Buzz'"]},"tags":["algo_design","easy"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L1.004","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"easy","algorithm_class":"library_use","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `most_common(xs: list[int]) -> int` returning the integer with highest frequency in xs (break ties by smaller value). Assume xs non-empty. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def most_common(xs):\n    from collections import Counter\n    c = Counter(xs)\n    best = None; best_cnt = -1\n    for v in sorted(c):\n        if c[v] > best_cnt:\n            best_cnt = c[v]; best = v\n    return best","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"most_common","tests":["assert most_common([1]) == 1","assert most_common([1, 2, 2, 3]) == 2","assert most_common([1, 1, 2, 2]) == 1","assert most_common([3, 3, 3, 1, 1]) == 3","assert most_common([5, 4, 3, 2, 1]) == 1","assert most_common([-1, -1, -2, -2]) == -2","assert most_common([0, 0, -1, -1, 2, 2]) == -1","assert most_common([10, 10, 10, -5, -5, -5, 0]) == -5","assert most_common([-3, -3, -3, -2, -2, -2, -1]) == -3","assert most_common([4, 4, 4, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 3]) == 1"]},"tags":["algo_design","easy"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L1.005","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"easy","algorithm_class":"greedy","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `reverse_words(s: str) -> str` returning s with the order of whitespace-separated words reversed and single-space-joined. e.g. 'a b c' -> 'c b a'. Empty/whitespace-only input returns ''. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def reverse_words(s: str) -> str:\n    return ' '.join(s.split()[::-1])","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"reverse_words","tests":["assert reverse_words('') == ''","assert reverse_words('   ') == ''","assert reverse_words('\\t\\n  ') == ''","assert reverse_words('hi') == 'hi'","assert reverse_words('a b c') == 'c b a'","assert reverse_words('  a   b  ') == 'b a'","assert reverse_words('a\\tb\\nc') == 'c b a'","assert reverse_words('  hello,   world!  again. ') == 'again. world! hello,'","assert reverse_words('one two three four five') == 'five four three two one'"]},"tags":["algo_design","easy"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L2.001","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"medium","algorithm_class":"DP","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write Python code defining `lis_length(xs: list[int]) -> int`, returning the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence of `xs`. The algorithm must run in O(n log n) time. Standard-library imports are allowed. Output only the code needed to define the function.","ground_truth":"import bisect\ndef lis_length(xs):\n    tails = []\n    for x in xs:\n        i = bisect.bisect_left(tails, x)\n        if i == len(tails): tails.append(x)\n        else: tails[i] = x\n    return len(tails)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert lis_length([]) == 0","assert lis_length([5]) == 1","assert lis_length([10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18]) == 4","assert lis_length([1,2,3,4]) == 4","assert lis_length([4,3,2,1]) == 1","assert lis_length([2,2,2]) == 1","assert lis_length([1,3,2,2,4]) == 3","assert lis_length([0,1,0,3,2,3]) == 4","assert lis_length(list(range(20000))) == 20000"]},"tags":["algo_design","dp"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L2.002","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"medium","algorithm_class":"graph_search","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `shortest_path_unweighted(graph: dict[int, list[int]], src: int, dst: int) -> int`. Treat every `v` in `graph[u]` as a directed edge `u -> v`; nodes absent as keys have no outgoing edges. Return the minimum number of edges from src to dst using BFS, or -1 if dst is unreachable. If src == dst, return 0. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"from collections import deque\ndef shortest_path_unweighted(graph, src, dst):\n    if src == dst: return 0\n    seen = {src}\n    q = deque([(src, 0)])\n    while q:\n        u, d = q.popleft()\n        for v in graph.get(u, []):\n            if v == dst: return d + 1\n            if v not in seen:\n                seen.add(v); q.append((v, d+1))\n    return -1","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"shortest_path_unweighted","tests":["assert shortest_path_unweighted({1:[2],2:[3],3:[]}, 1, 3) == 2","assert shortest_path_unweighted({1:[]}, 1, 1) == 0","assert shortest_path_unweighted({1:[2],2:[]}, 1, 3) == -1","assert shortest_path_unweighted({1:[2,3],2:[4],3:[4],4:[]}, 1, 4) == 2","assert shortest_path_unweighted({}, 1, 1) == 0","assert shortest_path_unweighted({1:[2],2:[3,4],3:[1],4:[]}, 1, 4) == 2","assert shortest_path_unweighted({1:[2,2],2:[3],3:[]}, 1, 3) == 2","assert shortest_path_unweighted({2:[3]}, 1, 3) == -1"]},"tags":["algo_design","bfs"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L2.003","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"medium","algorithm_class":"DP","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `coin_change(coins: list[int], amount: int) -> int` returning the minimum number of coins needed to make exactly `amount`, or `-1` if impossible. `coins` contains positive integers (and may be empty), and `amount` is a non-negative integer. Return `0` when `amount` is `0`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def coin_change(coins, amount):\n    INF = float('inf')\n    dp = [0] + [INF]*amount\n    for a in range(1, amount+1):\n        for c in coins:\n            if c <= a and dp[a-c] + 1 < dp[a]:\n                dp[a] = dp[a-c] + 1\n    return -1 if dp[amount] == INF else dp[amount]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"coin_change","tests":["assert coin_change([1,2,5], 11) == 3","assert coin_change([2], 3) == -1","assert coin_change([1], 0) == 0","assert coin_change([1,3,4], 6) == 2","assert coin_change([5], 10) == 2","assert coin_change([1,4,6], 8) == 2","assert coin_change([5,1,2,2], 11) == 3","assert coin_change([], 0) == 0","assert coin_change([], 7) == -1","assert coin_change([4,6], 7) == -1"]},"tags":["algo_design","dp"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L2.004","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"medium","algorithm_class":"math_or_NT","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function `next_prime(n: int) -> int` that returns the smallest prime strictly greater than `n`. `n` may be negative. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def next_prime(n):\n    def is_prime(k):\n        if k < 2: return False\n        if k % 2 == 0: return k == 2\n        i = 3\n        while i*i <= k:\n            if k % i == 0: return False\n            i += 2\n        return True\n    c = max(n, 1) + 1\n    while not is_prime(c): c += 1\n    return c","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert next_prime(-100) == 2","assert next_prime(-5) == 2","assert next_prime(0) == 2","assert next_prime(1) == 2","assert next_prime(2) == 3","assert next_prime(3) == 5","assert next_prime(8) == 11","assert next_prime(13) == 17","assert next_prime(20) == 23","assert next_prime(24) == 29","assert next_prime(48) == 53","assert next_prime(121) == 127","assert next_prime(997) == 1009","assert next_prime(1000000) == 1000003"]},"tags":["algo_design","prime"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L2.005","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"medium","algorithm_class":"greedy","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `meeting_rooms(intervals: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> int` returning the minimum number of rooms required so that no two overlapping meetings share a room. Each tuple is a valid meeting interval `(start, end)` with `start < end`; input may be in any order. Treat meetings as half-open intervals `[start, end)`, so a meeting ending at time `t` does not overlap one starting at time `t`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def meeting_rooms(intervals):\n    starts = sorted(i[0] for i in intervals)\n    ends = sorted(i[1] for i in intervals)\n    rooms = end_idx = 0\n    best = 0\n    for s in starts:\n        if s < ends[end_idx]:\n            rooms += 1\n        else:\n            end_idx += 1\n        if rooms > best: best = rooms\n    return best","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"meeting_rooms","tests":["assert meeting_rooms([]) == 0","assert meeting_rooms([(0,30)]) == 1","assert meeting_rooms([(0,30),(5,10),(15,20)]) == 2","assert meeting_rooms([(0,5),(5,10)]) == 1","assert meeting_rooms([(1,4),(2,5),(3,6)]) == 3","assert meeting_rooms([(5,10),(0,30),(15,20),(5,10)]) == 3","assert meeting_rooms([(0,10),(0,10),(0,10),(10,20)]) == 3","assert meeting_rooms([(0,1),(2,3),(2,4),(2,5)]) == 3","assert meeting_rooms([(-5,-1),(-3,2),(2,4)]) == 2","assert meeting_rooms([(1,2),(2,3),(3,4),(4,5)]) == 1"]},"tags":["algo_design","greedy"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L3.001","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"hard","algorithm_class":"DP","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `edit_distance(a: str, b: str) -> int` returning the standard Levenshtein distance between `a` and `b`, where insertion, deletion, and substitution each cost 1. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def edit_distance(a, b):\n    n, m = len(a), len(b)\n    dp = list(range(m+1))\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        prev = dp[0]; dp[0] = i\n        for j in range(1, m+1):\n            tmp = dp[j]\n            if a[i-1] == b[j-1]:\n                dp[j] = prev\n            else:\n                dp[j] = 1 + min(prev, dp[j], dp[j-1])\n            prev = tmp\n    return dp[m]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert edit_distance('', '') == 0","assert edit_distance('', 'abcd') == 4","assert edit_distance('abc', '') == 3","assert edit_distance('a', 'b') == 1","assert edit_distance('abc', 'abc') == 0","assert edit_distance('abc', 'xyz') == 3","assert edit_distance('abc', 'yabd') == 2","assert edit_distance('ab', 'ba') == 2","assert edit_distance('aaaa', 'aa') == 2","assert edit_distance('kitten', 'sitting') == 3","assert edit_distance('flaw', 'lawn') == 2","assert edit_distance('intention', 'execution') == 5","assert edit_distance('gumbo', 'gambol') == 2"]},"tags":["algo_design","dp","hard"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L3.002","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"hard","algorithm_class":"DP","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `min_extra_char(s: str, dictionary: list[str]) -> int`. Partition `s` into consecutive pieces. Each piece may either be a word in `dictionary` or a single uncovered character. Words from `dictionary` may be used multiple times. Return the minimum possible number of characters that are in uncovered-character pieces. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def min_extra_char(s, dictionary):\n    words = set(dictionary)\n    n = len(s)\n    dp = [0]*(n+1)\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + 1\n        for j in range(i):\n            if s[j:i] in words:\n                if dp[j] < dp[i]: dp[i] = dp[j]\n    return dp[n]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert min_extra_char('leetscode', ['leet', 'code', 'leetcode']) == 1","assert min_extra_char('sayhelloworld', ['hello', 'world']) == 3","assert min_extra_char('', ['a']) == 0","assert min_extra_char('abc', []) == 3","assert min_extra_char('abcdef', ['ab', 'cd', 'ef']) == 0","assert min_extra_char('ababa', ['aba', 'ab']) == 0","assert min_extra_char('aaaaab', ['aaaa', 'aaab']) == 2","assert min_extra_char('catsanddog', ['cat', 'cats', 'and', 'sand', 'dog']) == 0","assert min_extra_char('abcde', ['abc', 'bcd']) == 2","assert min_extra_char('aaaa', ['aa', 'aa']) == 0"]},"tags":["algo_design","dp","hard"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"medium","algorithm_class":"graph_search","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `dijkstra(graph: dict[int, list[tuple[int, int]]], src: int) -> dict[int, int]` that returns shortest-path distances from `src` in a directed graph. Every edge weight `w` is a non-negative integer. `graph[u]` is a list of `(v, w)` edges; nodes may appear only as edge destinations and need not be keys in `graph`. Treat `src` as a node even if it is absent from `graph`. Omit unreachable nodes from the result. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"import heapq\ndef dijkstra(graph, src):\n    dist = {src: 0}\n    h = [(0, src)]\n    while h:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(h)\n        if d > dist[u]: continue\n        for v, w in graph.get(u, []):\n            nd = d + w\n            if v not in dist or nd < dist[v]:\n                dist[v] = nd\n                heapq.heappush(h, (nd, v))\n    return dist","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"dijkstra","tests":["assert dijkstra({1:[(2,5),(3,1)],3:[(2,2)],2:[]}, 1) == {1:0, 3:1, 2:3}","assert dijkstra({1:[]}, 1) == {1:0}","assert dijkstra({1:[(2,1)],2:[(3,1)],3:[]}, 1) == {1:0,2:1,3:2}","assert 4 not in dijkstra({1:[(2,1)],2:[]}, 1)","assert dijkstra({1:[(1,5)],2:[]}, 1) == {1:0}","assert dijkstra({}, 7) == {7:0}","assert dijkstra({1:[(2,0),(3,5)],2:[(3,0)]}, 1) == {1:0,2:0,3:0}","assert dijkstra({1:[(2,10),(3,1)],3:[(2,1),(4,10)],2:[(4,1)],4:[(1,0)]}, 1) == {1:0,3:1,2:2,4:3}","assert dijkstra({1:[(2,4)]}, 1) == {1:0,2:4}"]},"tags":["algo_design","graph","hard"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"hard","algorithm_class":"math_or_NT","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `find_zero(coeffs: list[float]) -> float`. `coeffs` are coefficients of a polynomial in increasing-degree order (`coeffs[0] + coeffs[1]*x + coeffs[2]*x**2 + ...`). Return a real `x` such that `abs(p(x)) < 1e-3`. Test inputs guarantee that for some integer `k >= 0`, either `p(-2**k) == 0`, `p(2**k) == 0`, or `p(-2**k)` and `p(2**k)` have opposite signs. Locate such an interval by symmetric doubling, then use bisection. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def find_zero(coeffs):\n    def p(x):\n        value = 0.0\n        for c in reversed(coeffs):\n            value = value * x + c\n        return value\n\n    lo, hi = -1.0, 1.0\n    flo, fhi = p(lo), p(hi)\n    while flo != 0.0 and fhi != 0.0 and flo * fhi > 0.0:\n        lo *= 2.0\n        hi *= 2.0\n        flo, fhi = p(lo), p(hi)\n    if flo == 0.0:\n        return lo\n    if fhi == 0.0:\n        return hi\n    for _ in range(200):\n        mid = (lo + hi) / 2.0\n        fmid = p(mid)\n        if abs(fmid) < 1e-12:\n            return mid\n        if flo * fmid <= 0.0:\n            hi, fhi = mid, fmid\n        else:\n            lo, flo = mid, fmid\n    return (lo + hi) / 2.0","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["x = find_zero([0, 1]); assert abs(x) < 1e-5","x = find_zero([-2, 1]); assert abs(x - 2) < 1e-3","x = find_zero([-1, 0, 0, 1]); assert abs(x - 1) < 1e-3","x = find_zero([1, 0, -1]); assert abs(abs(x) - 1) < 1e-3","x = find_zero([2, -1, 0, 1]); assert abs(x**3 - x + 2) < 1e-3","x = find_zero([-8, 1]); assert abs(x - 8) < 1e-3","x = find_zero([1, 1]); assert abs(x + 1) < 1e-3"]},"tags":["algo_design","numerics","hard"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithm_design.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.algorithm_design","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"problem_difficulty":"hard","algorithm_class":"DP","input_format":"functional"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write only a Python function `longest_palindrome_subseq(s: str) -> int` that returns the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of `s`. Use dynamic programming with O(n^2) running time. O(n^2) or better auxiliary space is allowed.","ground_truth":"def longest_palindrome_subseq(s: str) -> int:\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                dp[i][j] = 2 if length == 2 else dp[i + 1][j - 1] + 2\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n    return dp[0][n - 1]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert longest_palindrome_subseq('') == 0","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('a') == 1","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('bbbab') == 4","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('cbbd') == 2","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('abcba') == 5","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('abcd') == 1","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('aaaa') == 4","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('agbdba') == 5","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('aebcbda') == 5","assert longest_palindrome_subseq('character') == 5"]},"tags":["algo_design","dp","hard"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"assertion_message","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing test:\n```\nAssertionError: add(2, 3) returned 6, expected 5\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a * b\n```\nFix the function to make the test pass while keeping the same signature. Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def add(a, b):\n    return a + b","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"add","tests":["assert add(2, 3) == 5","assert add(0, 0) == 0","assert add(-1, 1) == 0","assert add(100, 1) == 101","assert add(7, 8) == 15","assert add(1234, -567) == 667","assert add(-10, -20) == -30","assert add(42, 99) == 141"]},"tags":["tdd","fix"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"assertion_message","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing test:\n```\nAssertionError: count_evens([1,2,3,4]) returned 4, expected 2\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef count_evens(xs):\n    return len(xs)\n```\nFix `count_evens` so that it returns the number of even integers in `xs`, where `xs` is a list of integers. Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def count_evens(xs):\n    return sum(1 for x in xs if x % 2 == 0)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"count_evens","tests":["assert count_evens([1,2,3,4]) == 2","assert count_evens([]) == 0","assert count_evens([2,2,2]) == 3","assert count_evens([1,3,5]) == 0","assert count_evens([0,1,2]) == 2","assert count_evens([-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1]) == 3","assert count_evens([7,8,9,10,11,12]) == 3"]},"tags":["tdd","fix"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"assertion_message","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing test:\n```\nAssertionError: max_of([5,1,9,3]) returned 5, expected 9\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef max_of(xs):\n    return xs[0]\n```\nFix it so that it returns the largest value in a non-empty list of numbers. Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def max_of(xs):\n    m = xs[0]\n    for x in xs[1:]:\n        if x > m: m = x\n    return m","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"max_of","tests":["assert max_of([5,1,9,3]) == 9","assert max_of([1]) == 1","assert max_of([-1,-5,-3]) == -1","assert max_of([3,3,3]) == 3","assert max_of([100,1,2]) == 100","assert max_of([4,10,2,8]) == 10","assert max_of([1.5, 1.7, 1.6]) == 1.7"]},"tags":["tdd","fix"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L1.004","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"assertion_message","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing test:\n```\nAssertionError: starts_with('hello', 'he') returned False, expected True\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef starts_with(s, prefix):\n    return s == prefix\n```\nFix it. Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def starts_with(s, prefix):\n    return s[:len(prefix)] == prefix","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert starts_with('hello', 'he') == True","assert starts_with('hi', 'hi') == True","assert starts_with('a', 'ab') == False","assert starts_with('', '') == True","assert starts_with('abc', '') == True","assert starts_with('abc', 'b') == False","assert starts_with('abc', 'ac') == False","assert starts_with('abc', 'abd') == False"]},"tags":["tdd","fix"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L1.005","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"assertion_message","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing test:\n```\nAssertionError: factorial(5) returned 12000000, expected 120\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef factorial(n):\n    r = 1\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        r = r * i * 10\n    return r\n```\nFix it (remove the spurious *10). Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def factorial(n):\n    r = 1\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        r = r * i\n    return r","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"factorial","tests":["assert factorial(0) == 1","assert factorial(1) == 1","assert factorial(5) == 120","assert factorial(6) == 720","assert factorial(3) == 6"]},"tags":["tdd","fix"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L2.001","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"diff_output","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Multiple failing tests:\n```\nFAIL: roman(4) returned 'IIII', expected 'IV'\nFAIL: roman(9) returned 'VIIII', expected 'IX'\nFAIL: roman(40) returned 'XXXX', expected 'XL'\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef roman(n):\n    out = ''\n    for v, s in [(100,'C'),(50,'L'),(10,'X'),(5,'V'),(1,'I')]:\n        while n >= v: out += s; n -= v\n    return out\n```\nReplace it with a correct implementation of standard Roman numerals. `n` is an integer from 1 through 3999 inclusive; raise `ValueError` for any other input. Use subtractive forms IV, IX, XL, XC, CD, and CM. Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def roman(n):\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or isinstance(n, bool) or not 1 <= n <= 3999:\n        raise ValueError('n must be an integer from 1 to 3999')\n    table = [(1000, 'M'), (900, 'CM'), (500, 'D'), (400, 'CD'), (100, 'C'), (90, 'XC'), (50, 'L'), (40, 'XL'), (10, 'X'), (9, 'IX'), (5, 'V'), (4, 'IV'), (1, 'I')]\n    out = ''\n    for v, s in table:\n        while n >= v:\n            out += s\n            n -= v\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"roman","tests":["assert roman(1) == 'I'","assert roman(4) == 'IV'","assert roman(9) == 'IX'","assert roman(40) == 'XL'","assert roman(90) == 'XC'","assert roman(400) == 'CD'","assert roman(900) == 'CM'","assert roman(58) == 'LVIII'","assert roman(944) == 'CMXLIV'","assert roman(1994) == 'MCMXCIV'","assert roman(3999) == 'MMMCMXCIX'","for bad in (0, -1, 4000, 1.5, '4', True):\n    try:\n        roman(bad)\n        assert False, f'expected ValueError for {bad!r}'\n    except ValueError:\n        pass"]},"tags":["tdd","multi_fail"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L2.002","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"diff_output","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing tests:\n```\nFAIL: title_case('hello world') returned 'HELLO WORLD', expected 'Hello World'\nFAIL: title_case('') returned 'E', expected ''\nFAIL: title_case('a b c') returned 'ABC', expected 'A B C'\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef title_case(s):\n    return ''.join(c.upper() for c in s if c != ' ' or True) + 'E'\n```\nWrite a correct implementation that capitalises the first letter of every whitespace-separated word and lowercases the rest, joined by single spaces (matching how str.split/join work). Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def title_case(s):\n    return ' '.join(w.capitalize() for w in s.split())","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"title_case","tests":["assert title_case('') == ''","assert title_case('hello world') == 'Hello World'","assert title_case('a b c') == 'A B C'","assert title_case('HELLO') == 'Hello'","assert title_case('  spaced   words  ') == 'Spaced Words'","assert title_case('a\\tb\\nC') == 'A B C'","assert title_case(' \\t\\n ') == ''","assert title_case('hELLo woRLD') == 'Hello World'"]},"tags":["tdd","multi_fail"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L2.003","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"diff_output","iterations_needed":"1-2"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing tests:\n```\nFAIL: is_anagram('listen','silent') -> False, expected True\nFAIL: is_anagram('a','A') -> False, expected True\nFAIL: is_anagram('rat','car') -> True, expected False\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef is_anagram(a, b):\n    return a == b\n```\nWrite a correct implementation that's case-insensitive and ignores order. Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def is_anagram(a, b):\n    return sorted(a.lower()) == sorted(b.lower())","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"is_anagram","tests":["assert is_anagram('listen','silent') == True","assert is_anagram('a','A') == True","assert is_anagram('rat','car') == False","assert is_anagram('','') == True","assert is_anagram('aab','aba') == True","assert is_anagram('ab','a') == False","assert is_anagram('AaB','aba') == True"]},"tags":["tdd","multi_fail"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L2.004","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"diff_output","iterations_needed":"3-10"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing tests:\n```\nFAIL: parse_int('-5') -> 5, expected -5\nFAIL: parse_int('+10') -> raises, expected 10\nFAIL: parse_int(' 7 ') -> raises, expected 7\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef parse_int(s):\n    return int(s.lstrip('-'))\n```\nWrite a correct implementation. Remove surrounding whitespace first. After trimming, accept only an optional single ASCII `+` or `-` followed by one or more ASCII decimal digits (`0`-`9`); raise ValueError for every other string. Output only the corrected function.","ground_truth":"def parse_int(s):\n    s = s.strip()\n    if not s:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid integer\")\n    sign = \"\"\n    if s[0] in \"+-\":\n        sign, s = s[0], s[1:]\n    if not s or any(c < \"0\" or c > \"9\" for c in s):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid integer\")\n    return int(sign + s)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert parse_int('-5') == -5","assert parse_int('+10') == 10","assert parse_int(' 7 ') == 7","assert parse_int(' -0 ') == 0","assert parse_int('00012') == 12","for value in ['', '   ', '+', '-', '++1', '--1', '+-1', '1 2', '1.0', '1_000', 'abc']:\n    try:\n        parse_int(value)\n        assert False, repr(value)\n    except ValueError:\n        pass"]},"tags":["tdd","multi_fail"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L2.005","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"diff_output","iterations_needed":"3-10"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Failing tests:\n```\nFAIL: bsearch([1,3,5,7], 3) -> -1, expected 1\nFAIL: bsearch([1,3,5,7], 8) -> 3, expected -1\nFAIL: bsearch([], 0) -> raises, expected -1\n```\nBuggy code:\n```\ndef bsearch(xs, t):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(xs)\n    while lo < hi:\n        m = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if xs[m] < t: hi = m\n        else: lo = m + 1\n    return lo\n```\nWrite a correct implementation. `xs` is sorted in nondecreasing order. Return an index `i` such that `xs[i] == t`, or `-1` if `t` is not present. If duplicates exist, any matching index is acceptable. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def bsearch(xs, t):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(xs) - 1\n    while lo <= hi:\n        m = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if xs[m] == t: return m\n        if xs[m] < t: lo = m + 1\n        else: hi = m - 1\n    return -1","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"bsearch","tests":["assert bsearch([1,3,5,7], 3) == 1","assert bsearch([1,3,5,7], 8) == -1","assert bsearch([], 0) == -1","assert bsearch([1,3,5,7], 1) == 0","assert bsearch([1,3,5,7], 7) == 3","assert bsearch([2], 2) == 0","assert bsearch([2], 1) == -1","assert bsearch([-5,-1,0,4,9,12], 4) == 3","assert bsearch([-5,-1,0,4,9,12], 8) == -1","r = bsearch([1,2,2,2,3], 2); assert r in (1,2,3) and [1,2,2,2,3][r] == 2","assert bsearch(list(range(0, 100, 2)), 98) == 49","assert bsearch(list(range(0, 100, 2)), 99) == -1"]},"tags":["tdd","multi_fail"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L3.001","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"opaque_pass_fail","iterations_needed":"10+"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Hidden tests will exercise `serialize_form(d: dict) -> str`. Take a dict whose keys are strings and whose values are strings or integers. Return a custom form body: sort keys in ascending Python string order; emit each pair as `key=value`; and join pairs with `&`. Convert integer values with `str(value)`. Do not perform standard URL encoding on either keys or values: the only escaping rule is to replace every ASCII space (` `) in values with the literal string `%20`. All other characters, including `%`, `+`, `&`, `=`, non-ASCII characters, and spaces in keys, must remain unchanged. Return `''` for an empty dict. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def serialize_form(d):\n    parts = []\n    for k in sorted(d):\n        v = d[k]\n        if isinstance(v, int): v = str(v)\n        v = v.replace(' ', '%20')\n        parts.append(f'{k}={v}')\n    return '&'.join(parts)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert serialize_form({}) == ''","assert serialize_form({'a': '1'}) == 'a=1'","assert serialize_form({'b': '2', 'a': '1'}) == 'a=1&b=2'","assert serialize_form({'z': 'hi  there', 'a': 42}) == 'a=42&z=hi%20%20there'","assert serialize_form({'k': 'a+b%20&c=d'}) == 'k=a+b%20&c=d'","assert serialize_form({'a b': 'x y', 'A': 'é'}) == 'A=é&a b=x%20y'","assert serialize_form({'10': 'x', '2': 'y', '1': 'z'}) == '1=z&10=x&2=y'"]},"tags":["tdd","opaque"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L3.002","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"opaque_pass_fail","iterations_needed":"10+"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `merge_intervals(intervals)`. `intervals` is a list of `(start, end)` 2-tuples representing closed intervals, where `start <= end`. Return a new list of `(start, end)` 2-tuples in ascending order of start. Merge intervals that overlap or touch: an interval whose start is less than or equal to the previous merged interval's end must be merged. Do not mutate `intervals`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def merge_intervals(intervals):\n    if not intervals: return []\n    s = sorted(intervals)\n    out = [list(s[0])]\n    for a, b in s[1:]:\n        if a <= out[-1][1]:\n            out[-1][1] = max(out[-1][1], b)\n        else:\n            out.append([a, b])\n    return [tuple(x) for x in out]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert merge_intervals([]) == []","assert merge_intervals([(1,3),(2,6),(8,10)]) == [(1,6),(8,10)]","assert merge_intervals([(1,4),(4,5)]) == [(1,5)]","assert merge_intervals([(5,6),(1,2)]) == [(1,2),(5,6)]","assert merge_intervals([(1,10),(2,3),(4,5)]) == [(1,10)]","assert merge_intervals([(1,2),(2,3),(3,4)]) == [(1,4)]","assert merge_intervals([(1,2),(1,2),(2,2)]) == [(1,2)]","xs = [(3,5),(1,2)]; original = list(xs); result = merge_intervals(xs); assert xs == original and result is not xs"]},"tags":["tdd","opaque"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"opaque_pass_fail","iterations_needed":"10+"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Opaque tests for `parse_ini(text: str) -> dict[str, dict[str, str]]`. Spec: parse a simple INI format. For each line, first strip leading and trailing whitespace. Empty lines and lines whose stripped form begins with `;` are ignored. A stripped line of the form `[name]` selects section `name` exactly as written between the brackets; section names are case-sensitive. A line containing `=` is a key/value entry: split only at the first `=`, strip whitespace around the key and value, and store it in the current section. Before any section header, entries belong to `__default__`. Repeated section headers continue/merge the same section, and repeated keys are overwritten by the last occurrence. Any other nonempty line is ignored. Do not create a section dictionary merely because its header appears; empty input returns `{}`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def parse_ini(text):\n    out = {}\n    section = '__default__'\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        s = line.strip()\n        if not s or s.startswith(';'): continue\n        if s.startswith('[') and s.endswith(']'):\n            section = s[1:-1]\n            continue\n        if '=' in s:\n            k, v = s.split('=', 1)\n            out.setdefault(section, {})[k.strip()] = v.strip()\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"parse_ini","tests":["assert parse_ini('') == {}","assert parse_ini('[a]\\nk=v') == {'a': {'k': 'v'}}","assert parse_ini('k=v\\n[s]\\na=b') == {'__default__': {'k': 'v'}, 's': {'a': 'b'}}","assert parse_ini(';comment\\n[x]\\nfoo = bar ') == {'x': {'foo': 'bar'}}","assert parse_ini('[a]\\nk1=1\\nk2=2') == {'a': {'k1': '1', 'k2': '2'}}","assert parse_ini('  ; spaced comment\\n [ a ] \\n key = left=right ') == {' a ': {'key': 'left=right'}}","assert parse_ini('[a]\\nk=1\\n[a]\\nk=2') == {'a': {'k': '2'}}","assert parse_ini('[empty]') == {}","assert parse_ini('ignored line\\n[a]\\nx=y') == {'a': {'x': 'y'}}"]},"tags":["tdd","opaque"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"opaque_pass_fail","iterations_needed":"10+"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Opaque tests for `validate_iban(iban: str) -> bool`. Spec: simplified IBAN validator — strip spaces, must be uppercase letters and digits only, length 15..34, then move first 4 chars to the end, replace each letter with its 2-digit numeric value (A=10..Z=35), and check the resulting integer mod 97 == 1. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def validate_iban(iban):\n    s = iban.replace(' ', '')\n    if not 15 <= len(s) <= 34:\n        return False\n    if not all(('A' <= c <= 'Z') or ('0' <= c <= '9') for c in s):\n        return False\n    s = s[4:] + s[:4]\n    digits = ''\n    for c in s:\n        if '0' <= c <= '9':\n            digits += c\n        else:\n            digits += str(ord(c) - ord('A') + 10)\n    return int(digits) % 97 == 1","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert validate_iban('GB82WEST12345698765432') == True","assert validate_iban('GB82 WEST 1234 5698 7654 32') == True","assert validate_iban('GB82WEST12345698765431') == False","assert validate_iban('AB') == False","assert validate_iban('xx!!') == False","assert validate_iban('gb82west12345698765432') == False","assert validate_iban('Gb82WEST12345698765432') == False","assert validate_iban('GB82WÉST12345698765432') == False","assert validate_iban('GB82WEST1234569876543٢') == False"]},"tags":["tdd","opaque","validation"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.test_driven_iteration.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.test_driven_iteration","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"test_signal_quality":"opaque_pass_fail","iterations_needed":"10+"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Opaque tests for `topo_sort(graph: dict[str, list[str]]) -> list[str]`. Return a topological order over every node appearing as a key or in any adjacency list. At each step, among all not-yet-output nodes whose current in-degree is zero, choose the lexicographically smallest node. Raise `ValueError('cycle')` if any cycle exists. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def topo_sort(graph):\n    nodes = set(graph)\n    for vs in graph.values(): nodes.update(vs)\n    indeg = {n:0 for n in nodes}\n    for u, vs in graph.items():\n        for v in vs: indeg[v] += 1\n    import heapq\n    h = [n for n,d in indeg.items() if d==0]\n    heapq.heapify(h)\n    out = []\n    while h:\n        u = heapq.heappop(h)\n        out.append(u)\n        for v in graph.get(u, []):\n            indeg[v] -= 1\n            if indeg[v] == 0: heapq.heappush(h, v)\n    if len(out) != len(nodes): raise ValueError('cycle')\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert topo_sort({'a':['b'],'b':['c'],'c':[]}) == ['a','b','c']","assert topo_sort({'a':['c'],'b':['c'],'c':[]}) == ['a','b','c']","assert topo_sort({'b':[], 'a':[]}) == ['a','b']","assert topo_sort({'a':['c'], 'b':['d'], 'c':[], 'd':[]}) == ['a','b','c','d']","assert topo_sort({'a':['b']}) == ['a','b']","assert topo_sort({}) == []","assert topo_sort({'x':[]}) == ['x']","try:\n    topo_sort({'a':['a']})\n    assert False\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    assert str(e) == 'cycle'","try:\n    topo_sort({'a':['b'],'b':['c'],'c':['a']})\n    assert False\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    assert str(e) == 'cycle'"]},"tags":["tdd","opaque","graph"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"single","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `Counter` that displays a number starting at 0 and increments by 1 on each click of a single 'Increment' button. Use the React `useState` hook. Output only the component definition and any necessary imports.","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\nexport function Counter(){const [n, setN] = useState(0); return <div><span>{n}</span><button onClick={()=>setN(n+1)}>Increment</button></div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Counter"],"calls":["useState"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["useState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(\\s*0\\s*\\)","onClick\\s*=\\s*\\{"],"required_any_patterns":[["\\+\\s*1","\\+\\+"]],"pattern_counts":{"\\buseState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(":1},"required_jsx_patterns":[">\\s*Increment\\s*<"],"jsx_texts":["Increment"],"react_state_contract":{"initial":"0","event":"onClick","transition":"increment","display_state":true}}},"tags":["state","useState"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"single","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `Toggle` that displays the text 'on' or 'off' starting in 'off', with a single button labeled 'Flip' that toggles the state. Use useState. Output only the component (and imports).","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\nexport function Toggle(){const [on, setOn] = useState(false); return <div>{on?'on':'off'}<button onClick={()=>setOn(!on)}>Flip</button></div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Toggle"],"calls":["useState"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["useState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(\\s*false\\s*\\)","onClick\\s*=\\s*\\{"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{},"required_jsx_patterns":[">\\s*Flip\\s*<"],"jsx_texts":["Flip"],"react_state_contract":{"initial":"false","event":"onClick","transition":"toggle","display_state":true}}},"tags":["state","toggle"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"single","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `NameInput` with a single controlled `<input>` whose current value is also displayed live in a <p>. Use useState. Output only the component (and imports).","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\nexport function NameInput(){const [v, setV] = useState(''); return <div><input value={v} onChange={e=>setV(e.target.value)}/><p>{v}</p></div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["NameInput"],"calls":["useState"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["<input\\b[^>]*value\\s*=\\s*\\{","onChange\\s*=\\s*\\{[\\s\\S]*\\.target\\.value","<p\\b[^>]*>\\s*\\{\\s*\\w+\\s*\\}\\s*</p>"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{},"required_literal_patterns":["useState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(\\s*['\\\"]\\s*['\\\"]\\s*\\)"],"react_state_contract":{"initial":"empty_string","event":"onChange","transition":"target_value","display_state":true,"controlled_value":true}}},"tags":["state","controlled_input"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L1.004","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"single","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `LikeButton` that shows '❤ N' where N is the like count starting at 0; clicking the button increments N. Use useState. Output only the component (and imports).","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\n\nexport function LikeButton() {\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0);\n  return <button onClick={() => setN((prev) => prev + 1)}>❤ {n}</button>;\n}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["LikeButton"],"calls":["useState"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["useState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(\\s*0\\s*\\)","onClick\\s*=\\s*\\{"],"required_any_patterns":[["\\+\\s*1","\\+\\+"]],"pattern_counts":{},"jsx_texts":["❤"],"react_state_contract":{"initial":"0","event":"onClick","transition":"increment","display_state":true}}},"tags":["state","button"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L1.005","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"single","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `Visibility` that has a single 'Hide/Show' button toggling visibility of a child <p>Hello</p>. Initial state: visible. Use useState. Output only the component (and imports).","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\nexport function Visibility(){const [show, setShow] = useState(true); return <div><button onClick={()=>setShow(!show)}>{show?'Hide':'Show'}</button>{show && <p>Hello</p>}</div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Visibility"],"calls":["useState"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["useState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(\\s*true\\s*\\)","onClick\\s*=\\s*\\{"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{},"jsx_texts":["Hello"],"react_state_contract":{"initial":"true","event":"onClick","transition":"toggle","conditional_state":true}}},"tags":["state","conditional_render"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L2.001","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"few_independent","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `LoginForm` with two controlled inputs: one for `username` and one for `password` (the password input must use `type=\"password\"`). Store them in two separate `useState` slots initialized to empty strings. Derive `valid` as `username.length > 0 && password.length >= 4`, and disable the Submit button when `valid` is false. Output only the component and required imports.","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\nexport function LoginForm(){const [u, setU] = useState(''); const [p, setP] = useState(''); const valid = u.length > 0 && p.length >= 4; return <form><input value={u} onChange={e=>setU(e.target.value)} placeholder='username'/><input value={p} type='password' onChange={e=>setP(e.target.value)} placeholder='password'/><button disabled={!valid}>Submit</button></form>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["LoginForm"],"calls":["useState"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["\\.length\\s*>\\s*0\\s*&&\\s*\\w+\\.length\\s*>=\\s*4","disabled\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*!\\s*valid\\s*\\}"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{"\\buseState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(":2},"required_literal_patterns":["type\\s*=\\s*['\\\"]password['\\\"]"],"required_jsx_patterns":[">\\s*Submit\\s*<"],"jsx_texts":["Submit"],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"login_form"}}},"tags":["state","derived","controlled"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L2.002","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"interdependent","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `TodoList` component with a controlled text input and a list-of-items state. Pressing `Add` appends the input's current value to the list only if it is non-empty, then clears the input. Each list item must have a small `x` button that removes that item. Use exactly two `useState` calls. Output only the component and any required imports.","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\nexport function TodoList(){const [items, setItems] = useState<string[]>([]); const [text, setText] = useState(''); return <div><input value={text} onChange={e=>setText(e.target.value)}/><button onClick={()=>{if(text){setItems([...items, text]); setText('');}}}>Add</button><ul>{items.map((it,i)=>(<li key={i}>{it}<button onClick={()=>setItems(items.filter((_,j)=>j!==i))}>x</button></li>))}</ul></div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["TodoList"],"calls":["useState","map","filter"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["onChange\\s*=\\s*\\{","onClick\\s*=\\s*\\{"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{"\\buseState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(":2},"required_literal_patterns":["set\\w+\\s*\\(\\s*['\\\"]\\s*['\\\"]\\s*\\)"],"required_jsx_patterns":[">\\s*Add\\s*<",">\\s*x\\s*<"],"jsx_texts":["Add","x"],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"todo_list"}}},"tags":["state","list","add_remove"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L2.003","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"few_independent","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `Stepper` component with numeric state `min`, `max`, and `v`, initially 0, 10, and 0. Render two `<input type=\"number\">` controls for `min` and `max`, display `v`, and render '-' and '+' buttons. Maintain the invariant `min <= max` and `min <= v <= max` after every user action. When min is edited above max, set max to the new min; when max is edited below min, set min to the new max. After either boundary changes, clamp v to the resulting range. The '-' and '+' buttons decrement/increment v by 1 without leaving the range. Output only the component and imports.","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\n\nexport function Stepper() {\n  const [min, setMin] = useState(0);\n  const [max, setMax] = useState(10);\n  const [v, setV] = useState(0);\n\n  const changeMin = (nextMin: number) => {\n    const nextMax = Math.max(max, nextMin);\n    setMin(nextMin);\n    setMax(nextMax);\n    setV(current => Math.min(nextMax, Math.max(nextMin, current)));\n  };\n\n  const changeMax = (nextMax: number) => {\n    const nextMin = Math.min(min, nextMax);\n    setMin(nextMin);\n    setMax(nextMax);\n    setV(current => Math.min(nextMax, Math.max(nextMin, current)));\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <input type=\"number\" value={min} onChange={e => changeMin(e.currentTarget.valueAsNumber)} />\n      <input type=\"number\" value={max} onChange={e => changeMax(e.currentTarget.valueAsNumber)} />\n      <button onClick={() => setV(current => Math.max(min, current - 1))}>-</button>\n      <span>{v}</span>\n      <button onClick={() => setV(current => Math.min(max, current + 1))}>+</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Stepper"],"calls":["useState"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["onChange\\s*=\\s*\\{","onClick\\s*=\\s*\\{","(?:newMin\\s*>\\s*max|Math\\.max\\s*\\()","(?:newMax\\s*<\\s*min|Math\\.min\\s*\\()"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{"\\buseState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(":3},"required_literal_patterns":["type\\s*=\\s*[\\'\\\"]number[\\'\\\"]"],"required_jsx_patterns":[">\\s*-\\s*<",">\\s*\\+\\s*<"],"jsx_texts":["-","+"],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"stepper"}}},"tags":["state","clamp"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L2.004","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"few_independent","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `Tabs` component that takes non-empty `tabs: string[]` and `panels: ReactNode[]` props of equal length. Use exactly one `useState` call to store the active tab's numeric index, initially 0. Render a button for each tab; clicking its button must set that tab's index as active. Render only the panel at the active index. Output only the component and necessary imports.","ground_truth":"import { useState, type ReactNode } from 'react';\n\nexport function Tabs({ tabs, panels }: { tabs: string[]; panels: ReactNode[] }) {\n  const [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState(0);\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <div role=\"tablist\">\n        {tabs.map((tab, index) => (\n          <button\n            key={index}\n            type=\"button\"\n            aria-selected={activeIndex === index}\n            onClick={() => setActiveIndex(index)}\n          >\n            {tab}\n          </button>\n        ))}\n      </div>\n      <div>{panels[activeIndex]}</div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Tabs"],"calls":["useState","map"],"string_literals":[],"required_patterns":["useState(?:\\s*<\\s*number\\s*>)?\\s*\\(\\s*0\\s*\\)","onClick\\s*=\\s*\\{[\\s\\S]*\\(\\s*index\\s*\\)","panels\\s*\\[\\s*activeIndex\\s*\\]"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{"\\buseState(?:\\s*<[^>]+>)?\\s*\\(":1},"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"tabs"}}},"tags":["state","tabs"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L2.005","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"interdependent","lifecycle":"pure_state"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `Cart` component. Define `Item` as `{ id: string; qty: number; price: number }` and keep `items` as component state. Render an “Add a” button that adds one unit of product `{ id: 'a', price: 5 }`: if item `a` already exists, increment its `qty`; otherwise append it with `qty: 1`. Render each item with a Remove button that deletes the entire item matching that item's id. On every render, derive `total` from `items` by summing `item.qty * item.price`; do not store total in separate state. Output only the component and imports.","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\ntype Item = {id:string; qty:number; price:number};\nexport function Cart(){const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([]); const total = items.reduce((s,i)=>s + i.qty*i.price, 0); const add=(id:string,p:number)=>setItems(prev=>{const e = prev.find(x=>x.id===id); return e ? prev.map(x=>x.id===id?{...x, qty:x.qty+1}:x) : [...prev, {id, qty:1, price:p}];}); const remove=(id:string)=>setItems(prev=>prev.filter(x=>x.id!==id)); return <div><div>Total: {total}</div><button onClick={()=>add('a',5)}>add a</button><ul>{items.map(i=>(<li key={i.id}>{i.id} x{i.qty} <button onClick={()=>remove(i.id)}>rm</button></li>))}</ul></div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Cart"],"required_any_patterns":[["\\btype\\s+Item\\s*=\\s*\\{(?=[^}]*\\bid\\s*:\\s*string\\b)(?=[^}]*\\bqty\\s*:\\s*number\\b)(?=[^}]*\\bprice\\s*:\\s*number\\b)[^}]*\\}","\\binterface\\s+Item\\s*\\{(?=[^}]*\\bid\\s*:\\s*string\\b)(?=[^}]*\\bqty\\s*:\\s*number\\b)(?=[^}]*\\bprice\\s*:\\s*number\\b)[^}]*\\}"]],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"cart"},"component_contract":{"binding":"Cart","return_checks":{"required_patterns":["\\bonClick\\s*=\\s*\\{","\\b[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*\\s*\\.\\s*map\\s*\\("]}}}},"tags":["state","cart","derived"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L3.001","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"interdependent","lifecycle":"effect_with_cleanup"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `Clock` component that displays the current local time strictly as `HH:MM:SS` (two-digit hours, minutes, and seconds), updating once per second. Use `useState` for the current time and `useEffect` with `setInterval`; the cleanup function returned from `useEffect` must call `clearInterval` for that interval. Output only the component and required imports.","ground_truth":"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\n\nexport function Clock() {\n  const [time, setTime] = useState(() => new Date());\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const intervalId = setInterval(() => setTime(new Date()), 1000);\n    return () => clearInterval(intervalId);\n  }, []);\n\n  const hh = String(time.getHours()).padStart(2, '0');\n  const mm = String(time.getMinutes()).padStart(2, '0');\n  const ss = String(time.getSeconds()).padStart(2, '0');\n\n  return <div>{`${hh}:${mm}:${ss}`}</div>;\n}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Clock"],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"clock"},"component_contract":{"binding":"Clock","return_checks":{"required_patterns":["<[A-Za-z][\\w.]*\\b"]}}}},"tags":["state","effect","cleanup"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L3.002","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"interdependent","lifecycle":"effect_with_cleanup"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Assume this component runs in a browser client environment. Write a React TSX `WindowSize` component that displays the current window width and height. Use exactly one `useState` slot whose value is an object with `w` and `h` fields. In `useEffect`, subscribe to `window`'s `'resize'` event and return a cleanup function that unsubscribes the same listener. Output only the component and required imports.","ground_truth":"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\nexport function WindowSize(){const [s, setS] = useState({w: window.innerWidth, h: window.innerHeight}); useEffect(()=>{const onR=()=>setS({w: window.innerWidth, h: window.innerHeight}); window.addEventListener('resize', onR); return ()=>window.removeEventListener('resize', onR);}, []); return <div>{s.w} x {s.h}</div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["WindowSize"],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"window_size"},"component_contract":{"binding":"WindowSize","return_checks":{"required_patterns":["\\{\\s*[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*\\.w\\s*\\}[\\s\\S]*\\{\\s*[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*\\.h\\s*\\}"]}}}},"tags":["state","subscription","cleanup"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"interdependent","lifecycle":"effects"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `Search` component. An input must control `query`. Use a debounced effect: after 300ms, set `debounced` from `query`, and clear that timer in the effect cleanup. Use a second effect that runs when `debounced` changes; only when `debounced` is non-empty, fetch results with `fetch('/api?q=' + debounced, { signal })`. This fetch effect must create an `AbortController`, pass its `signal` to `fetch`, and call `abort()` in cleanup. Output only the component and imports.","ground_truth":"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\nexport function Search(){const [query, setQuery] = useState(''); const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(''); const [results, setResults] = useState<any[]>([]); useEffect(()=>{const id = setTimeout(()=>setDebounced(query), 300); return ()=>clearTimeout(id);}, [query]); useEffect(()=>{if(!debounced) return; const ac = new AbortController(); fetch('/api?q='+debounced, {signal: ac.signal}).then(r=>r.json()).then(setResults).catch(()=>{}); return ()=>ac.abort();}, [debounced]); return <div><input value={query} onChange={e=>setQuery(e.target.value)}/><ul>{results.map((r:any,i:number)=>(<li key={i}>{r}</li>))}</ul></div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Search"],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"debounced_search"},"component_contract":{"binding":"Search","return_checks":{"required_patterns":["<input\\b(?=[^>]*\\bvalue\\s*=\\s*\\{)(?=[^>]*\\bonChange\\s*=\\s*\\{)","\\b[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*\\s*\\.\\s*map\\s*\\("]}}}},"tags":["state","debounce","abort"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"interdependent","lifecycle":"effects"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `Drag` component that renders a draggable box and stores its `{ x, y }` position with `useState`. On mousedown, compute and store the pointer offset from the box's current position and begin dragging (a nullable offset state may be used to represent dragging, or you may use separate dragging and offset state). While dragging, use `useEffect` to attach `mousemove` and `mouseup` listeners to `window`; mousemove must update the box position as pointer coordinates minus the stored offset, and mouseup must stop dragging. The effect cleanup must remove both listeners. Output only imports and the component.","ground_truth":"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\nexport function Drag(){const [pos, setPos] = useState({x:0,y:0}); const [drag, setDrag] = useState<{ox:number;oy:number}|null>(null); useEffect(()=>{if(!drag) return; const mm=(e:MouseEvent)=>setPos({x: e.clientX - drag.ox, y: e.clientY - drag.oy}); const mu=()=>setDrag(null); window.addEventListener('mousemove', mm); window.addEventListener('mouseup', mu); return ()=>{window.removeEventListener('mousemove', mm); window.removeEventListener('mouseup', mu);};}, [drag]); return <div onMouseDown={e=>setDrag({ox:e.clientX-pos.x, oy:e.clientY-pos.y})} style={{position:'absolute', left:pos.x, top:pos.y, width:100, height:100, background:'#08f'}}/>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Drag"],"calls":["useState","useEffect"],"string_literals":["mousemove","mouseup"],"required_patterns":["onMouseDown\\s*=\\s*\\{","clientX\\s*-\\s*\\w+\\.x","clientY\\s*-\\s*\\w+\\.y"],"required_any_patterns":[],"pattern_counts":{"addEventListener\\s*\\(":2,"removeEventListener\\s*\\(":2},"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"drag"}}},"tags":["state","drag","listeners"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.state_management.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.state_management","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"num_state_slots":"interdependent","lifecycle":"effects"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX `Poller` component that polls `/api/status` every 5s only while the document is visible. On mount, do not create an interval if `document.hidden` is true. Use `document.visibilitychange` to stop polling when hidden and resume polling when visible. A `useEffect` must register the visibilitychange listener and manage an interval id, cleaning up both the listener and any active interval on unmount. State must hold the latest status string returned by the endpoint. Output only the component and imports.","ground_truth":"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\n\nexport function Poller() {\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState('');\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    let id: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null = null;\n\n    const poll = () => {\n      fetch('/api/status')\n        .then((response) => response.text())\n        .then(setStatus);\n    };\n\n    const start = () => {\n      if (id === null) {\n        id = setInterval(poll, 5000);\n      }\n    };\n\n    const stop = () => {\n      if (id !== null) {\n        clearInterval(id);\n        id = null;\n      }\n    };\n\n    const onVisibilityChange = () => {\n      if (document.hidden) {\n        stop();\n      } else {\n        start();\n      }\n    };\n\n    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibilityChange);\n    onVisibilityChange();\n\n    return () => {\n      stop();\n      document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibilityChange);\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  return <div>{status}</div>;\n}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Poller"],"react_behavior_contract":{"kind":"visibility_poller"},"component_contract":{"binding":"Poller","return_checks":{"required_patterns":["<[A-Za-z][\\w.]*\\b"]}}}},"tags":["state","poll","visibility"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"quadratic_to_linear","data_structure_invention":"pick_from_stdlib"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Reference (slow): `def has_dup(xs): return any(xs[i] == xs[j] for i in range(len(xs)) for j in range(i + 1, len(xs)))` uses nested pairwise comparisons and takes O(n²) time. Rewrite `has_dup(xs: list[int]) -> bool` using a `set` of previously seen integers. The implementation must run in expected/average O(n) time. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def has_dup(xs: list[int]) -> bool:\n    seen = set()\n    for x in xs:\n        if x in seen:\n            return True\n        seen.add(x)\n    return False","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert has_dup([]) is False","assert has_dup([1]) is False","assert has_dup([1, 2, 3]) is False","assert has_dup([1, 2, 1]) is True","assert has_dup([0, -1, 2, -1]) is True"]},"tags":["optimize","set"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"quadratic_to_linear","data_structure_invention":"pick_from_stdlib"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Reference (slow): `def common(a, b): return [x for x in a if x in b]` where `b` is a list — O(|a||b|). Rewrite `common(a: list[int], b: list[int]) -> list[int]` by converting `b` to a set, so membership tests are average O(1) and the overall average running time is O(|a| + |b|). Preserve the order and duplicates from `a`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def common(a: list[int], b: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    b_set = set(b)\n    return [x for x in a if x in b_set]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"common","tests":["assert common([], []) == []","assert common([1, 2, 3], [2, 3, 4]) == [2, 3]","assert common([1, 1, 2], [1]) == [1, 1]","assert common([1, 2, 3], []) == []","a = list(range(50000)); b = list(range(40000, 90000)); assert len(common(a, b)) == 10000"],"source_checks":{"function":"common","calls":["set"]}},"tags":["optimize","set"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"quadratic_to_linear","data_structure_invention":"pick_from_stdlib"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Reference (slow): repeatedly inserts elements at the front of a list, causing O(n^2) work. Implement `reverse_collect(xs: list[int]) -> list[int]` that returns a new list containing `xs` in reverse order using O(n) time. You may use slicing, `reversed`, or `collections.deque`; do not repeatedly insert or prepend into a list. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def reverse_collect(xs: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    return xs[::-1]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert reverse_collect([]) == []","assert reverse_collect([1]) == [1]","assert reverse_collect([1, 2, 3]) == [3, 2, 1]","xs = list(range(100000)); result = reverse_collect(xs); assert result == xs[::-1]; assert result is not xs"]},"tags":["optimize","linear"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L1.004","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"quadratic_to_linear","data_structure_invention":"pick_from_stdlib"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Rewrite `freq(xs: list[int]) -> dict[int, int]` so that it returns the frequency of every integer in `xs`. Use a plain dictionary as a hash counter and make exactly one pass over `xs`; do not sort and do not call `count`. Output only the function definition.","ground_truth":"def freq(xs: list[int]) -> dict[int, int]:\n    out: dict[int, int] = {}\n    for x in xs:\n        out[x] = out.get(x, 0) + 1\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert freq([]) == {}","assert freq([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]) == {1: 1, 2: 2, 3: 3}","assert freq([-1, 0, -1, 10, 0, 0]) == {-1: 2, 0: 3, 10: 1}","xs = list(range(200000)); xs.extend(xs); r = freq(xs); assert len(r) == 200000 and r[0] == 2 and r[199999] == 2"]},"tags":["optimize","hash"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L1.005","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"quadratic_to_linear","data_structure_invention":"pick_from_stdlib"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Rewrite `repeat_join(words: list[str], sep: str) -> str` so that it returns all strings in `words` joined by `sep`. You must use `str.join` (for example, `sep.join(words)`). Its running time should be linear in the total output character length, including inserted separators. Output only the function, including the stated type annotations.","ground_truth":"def repeat_join(words: list[str], sep: str) -> str:\n    return sep.join(words)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert repeat_join([], ',') == ''","assert repeat_join(['a'], ',') == 'a'","assert repeat_join(['a', 'b', 'c'], '-') == 'a-b-c'","assert repeat_join(['x', 'y'], '') == 'xy'","assert repeat_join(['ab', '', 'cd'], '🙂') == 'ab🙂🙂cd'"]},"tags":["optimize","string"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L2.001","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"well_known_algorithm"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Rewrite `inner_join(a: list[tuple[int, int]], b: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> list[tuple[int, int, int]]` to return every `(key, av, bv)` such that `(key, av)` is in `a` and `(key, bv)` is in `b`. Preserve duplicate matches: if a key occurs m times in `a` and n times in `b`, return m*n tuples for that key. Use a hash join by building a dictionary from keys in `b`, for expected O(|a| + |b|) time. Output only the function. Output order is not significant.","ground_truth":"def inner_join(a: list[tuple[int, int]], b: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> list[tuple[int, int, int]]:\n    idx: dict[int, list[int]] = {}\n    for k, bv in b:\n        idx.setdefault(k, []).append(bv)\n    out: list[tuple[int, int, int]] = []\n    for k, av in a:\n        for bv in idx.get(k, []):\n            out.append((k, av, bv))\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert inner_join([], []) == []","assert sorted(inner_join([(1, 7)], [(1, 9)])) == [(1, 7, 9)]","assert inner_join([(1, 1)], [(2, 2)]) == []","assert sorted(inner_join([(1, 1), (2, 3), (1, 2)], [(1, 10), (3, 30), (1, 20), (2, 40)])) == [(1, 1, 10), (1, 1, 20), (1, 2, 10), (1, 2, 20), (2, 3, 40)]","assert sorted(inner_join([(5, 1), (5, 1)], [(5, 2), (5, 2)])) == [(5, 1, 2), (5, 1, 2), (5, 1, 2), (5, 1, 2)]"]},"tags":["optimize","hash_join"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L2.002","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"well_known_algorithm"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Rewrite `range_sums(xs: list[int], queries: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> list[int]` using a prefix-sum table. Each query `(l, r)` is inclusive, and all queries are guaranteed valid: `0 <= l <= r < len(xs)`; if `xs` is empty, `queries` is empty. Return the sum for each query in order. Target total time is O(n + q), where n = len(xs) and q = len(queries). Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def range_sums(xs, queries):\n    pre = [0]\n    for x in xs: pre.append(pre[-1] + x)\n    return [pre[r+1] - pre[l] for l, r in queries]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert range_sums([], []) == []","assert range_sums([1, 2, 3, 4], [(0, 3), (1, 2), (2, 2)]) == [10, 5, 3]","assert range_sums([-5, 0, 7, -2, 4], [(0, 0), (0, 4), (1, 3), (3, 4)]) == [-5, 4, 5, 2]","xs = list(range(-100, 101)); qs = [(0, 200), (50, 150), (99, 101), (200, 200)]; assert range_sums(xs, qs) == [sum(xs[0:201]), sum(xs[50:151]), sum(xs[99:102]), sum(xs[200:201])]","import time; xs = list(range(200000)); qs = [(0, 199999), (50000, 150000), (123, 45678), (199999, 199999)] * 2500; expected = [19999900000, 10000100000, 1043255178, 199999] * 2500; t = time.perf_counter(); got = range_sums(xs, qs); elapsed = time.perf_counter() - t; assert got == expected; assert elapsed < 2.0"]},"tags":["optimize","prefix_sum"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L2.003","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"well_known_algorithm"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Reference (slow): linear scan of a sorted list per lookup — O(n) per call. Implement `count_le(sorted_xs: list[int], v: int) -> int`, returning the number of elements `<= v` in the sorted list using binary search in O(log n) time. Duplicates must be counted. You may use the Python standard library. Output only Python code defining the function.","ground_truth":"def count_le(sorted_xs: list[int], v: int) -> int:\n    import bisect\n    return bisect.bisect_right(sorted_xs, v)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert count_le([], 0) == 0","assert count_le([1, 2, 3], 2) == 2","assert count_le([1, 2, 3], 0) == 0","assert count_le([1, 2, 3], 5) == 3","assert count_le([1, 2, 2, 2, 3], 2) == 4","assert count_le([7, 7, 7], 7) == 3","class SpyInt(int):\n    comparisons = 0\n    @classmethod\n    def reset(cls):\n        cls.comparisons = 0\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        type(self).comparisons += 1\n        return super().__lt__(other)\n    def __le__(self, other):\n        type(self).comparisons += 1\n        return super().__le__(other)\n    def __gt__(self, other):\n        type(self).comparisons += 1\n        return super().__gt__(other)\n    def __ge__(self, other):\n        type(self).comparisons += 1\n        return super().__ge__(other)\nxs = [SpyInt(i) for i in range(1 << 16)]\nSpyInt.reset()\nassert count_le(xs, SpyInt(40000)) == 40001\nassert SpyInt.comparisons < 100"]},"tags":["optimize","binary_search"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L2.004","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"well_known_algorithm"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `sliding_max_avg(xs: list[float], k: int) -> float`. For a non-empty `xs` and `1 <= k <= len(xs)`, return the maximum arithmetic mean among all contiguous windows of exactly `k` elements. Raise `ValueError` if `k <= 0` or `k > len(xs)`. Use a running window sum: initialize the first window once, then update it by adding the incoming value and subtracting the outgoing value. The algorithm must use O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def sliding_max_avg(xs: list[float], k: int) -> float:\n    if k <= 0 or k > len(xs):\n        raise ValueError(\"k must satisfy 1 <= k <= len(xs)\")\n    window_sum = sum(xs[:k])\n    best_sum = window_sum\n    for i in range(k, len(xs)):\n        window_sum += xs[i] - xs[i - k]\n        if window_sum > best_sum:\n            best_sum = window_sum\n    return best_sum / k","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert sliding_max_avg([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 1) == 5.0","assert sliding_max_avg([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 5) == 3.0","assert sliding_max_avg([1, 12, -5, -6, 50, 3], 4) == 12.75","assert abs(sliding_max_avg([1.5, 2.5, -1.0, 4.0], 2) - 2.0) < 1e-12","assert sliding_max_avg([-8, -2, -5, -3], 2) == -3.5","assert sliding_max_avg([5, 5, 5, 5], 2) == 5.0","for bad_k in (0, -1, 6):\n    try:\n        sliding_max_avg([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], bad_k)\n        raise AssertionError('expected ValueError')\n    except ValueError:\n        pass"]},"tags":["optimize","sliding_window"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L2.005","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"well_known_algorithm"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Rewrite `max_subarray(xs: list[int]) -> int` using Kadane's algorithm in O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. For a non-empty input, return the maximum sum over all non-empty contiguous subarrays. If `xs` is empty, return 0. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def max_subarray(xs: list[int]) -> int:\n    if not xs:\n        return 0\n    best = cur = xs[0]\n    for x in xs[1:]:\n        cur = max(x, cur + x)\n        best = max(best, cur)\n    return best","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert max_subarray([]) == 0","assert max_subarray([0]) == 0","assert max_subarray([-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]) == 6","assert max_subarray([1, 2, 3]) == 6","assert max_subarray([-1, -2, -3]) == -1","assert max_subarray([0, -1, 0]) == 0","assert max_subarray([5, -10, 6]) == 6","assert max_subarray([-1, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]) == 6","import time; xs = list(range(50000)) + [-10**9]; t = time.perf_counter(); assert max_subarray(xs) == sum(range(50000)); assert time.perf_counter() - t < 3.0"]},"tags":["optimize","kadane"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L3.001","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"novel_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `count_occurrences(text: str, patterns: list[str]) -> dict[str, int]`. Each pattern is a distinct, non-empty string. Return a dictionary mapping every supplied pattern to the number of its (overlapping) occurrences in `text`; return 0 for absent patterns, and return {} when `patterns` is empty. Build a trie/automaton over all patterns and scan `text` once (Aho-Corasick or an equivalent multi-pattern automaton). Required complexity: O(|text| + sum(len(p) for p in patterns) + matches), excluding the returned dictionary size. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def count_occurrences(text, patterns):\n    from collections import deque\n    trie = [{}]; out = [[]]; fail = [0]\n    for p in patterns:\n        node = 0\n        for c in p:\n            if c not in trie[node]:\n                trie.append({}); out.append([]); fail.append(0)\n                trie[node][c] = len(trie)-1\n            node = trie[node][c]\n        out[node].append(p)\n    q = deque()\n    for c, nxt in trie[0].items():\n        fail[nxt] = 0; q.append(nxt)\n    while q:\n        u = q.popleft()\n        for c, v in trie[u].items():\n            q.append(v)\n            f = fail[u]\n            while f and c not in trie[f]: f = fail[f]\n            fail[v] = trie[f].get(c, 0) if trie[f].get(c, 0) != v else 0\n            out[v].extend(out[fail[v]])\n    counts = {p:0 for p in patterns}\n    node = 0\n    for c in text:\n        while node and c not in trie[node]: node = fail[node]\n        node = trie[node].get(c, 0)\n        for m in out[node]: counts[m] += 1\n    return counts","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert count_occurrences('abcabc', ['abc']) == {'abc': 2}","assert count_occurrences('aaa', ['aa']) == {'aa': 2}","assert count_occurrences('hello', ['hi', 'he', 'lo']) == {'hi': 0, 'he': 1, 'lo': 1}","assert count_occurrences('', ['x']) == {'x': 0}","assert count_occurrences('abcde', []) == {}","assert count_occurrences('ushers', ['he', 'she', 'his', 'hers']) == {'he': 1, 'she': 1, 'his': 0, 'hers': 1}","assert count_occurrences('aaaaa', ['a', 'aa', 'aaa', 'aaaa']) == {'a': 5, 'aa': 4, 'aaa': 3, 'aaaa': 2}"]},"tags":["optimize","multi_pattern","novel"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L3.002","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"novel_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Rewrite `kth_smallest_stream(xs: list[int], k: int) -> list[int]`. Let `k` be a positive integer. Return one value for each prefix `xs[0..i]` whose length is at least `k`: the k-th smallest element of that prefix (counting duplicates). If `k > len(xs)`, return `[]`; raise `ValueError` if `k <= 0`. Target O(n log k) time and O(k) auxiliary space by maintaining a max-heap containing the k smallest values seen so far. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def kth_smallest_stream(xs, k):\n    import heapq\n    if k <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"k must be positive\")\n    out = []\n    h = []  # negated max-heap containing the k smallest seen values\n    for x in xs:\n        if len(h) < k:\n            heapq.heappush(h, -x)\n        elif x < -h[0]:\n            heapq.heapreplace(h, -x)\n        if len(h) == k:\n            out.append(-h[0])\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert kth_smallest_stream([], 3) == []","assert kth_smallest_stream([5,3,1,4,2], 2) == [5,3,3,2]","assert kth_smallest_stream([2,2,1,1], 2) == [2,2,1]","assert kth_smallest_stream([-1,-3,2,-2], 3) == [2,-1]","assert kth_smallest_stream([1,2,3,4], 1) == [1,1,1,1]","assert kth_smallest_stream([1,2,3,4], 4) == [4]","assert kth_smallest_stream([1,2], 3) == []","try:\n    kth_smallest_stream([1], 0)\n    assert False\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass","import time; t=time.time(); xs=list(range(50000,0,-1)); r=kth_smallest_stream(xs, 10); assert r[-1] == 10 and len(r) == 49991; assert time.time()-t < 5.0"]},"tags":["optimize","heap"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"novel_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Given n >= 0 and a list ops of valid operations (l, r, v), where each operation satisfies 0 <= l <= r < n and denotes adding v to every index in the inclusive range [l, r], implement `range_add(n: int, ops: list[tuple[int, int, int]]) -> list[int]`. Return the final length-n array initialized with zeros. Use a one-dimensional difference array so the total running time is O(n + len(ops)) and extra space is O(n). Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def range_add(n: int, ops: list[tuple[int, int, int]]) -> list[int]:\n    diff = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for l, r, v in ops:\n        diff[l] += v\n        diff[r + 1] -= v\n    result = [0] * n\n    running = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        running += diff[i]\n        result[i] = running\n    return result","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert range_add(5, []) == [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]","assert range_add(5, [(0, 4, 1)]) == [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]","assert range_add(5, [(1, 3, 2), (0, 4, 1)]) == [1, 3, 3, 3, 1]","assert range_add(1, [(0, 0, 7)]) == [7]","assert range_add(6, [(0, 0, -2), (5, 5, 3), (1, 4, -1)]) == [-2, -1, -1, -1, -1, 3]","assert range_add(7, [(1, 5, 4), (2, 6, -3), (3, 3, 10)]) == [0, 4, 1, 11, 1, 1, -3]","import time; ops = [(i % 50000, 99999, 1) for i in range(50000)]; t = time.time(); result = range_add(100000, ops); assert result[0] == 1 and result[-1] == 50000 and time.time() - t < 3.0"]},"tags":["optimize","difference_array"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"novel_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Reference (slow): scan xs[0..i] for each prefix-max query. Rewrite `point_update_prefix_max(n: int, ops: list[tuple[Literal[\"set\"], int, int] | tuple[Literal[\"qry\"], int]]) -> list[int]` using a segment tree. Each operation is either `('set', i, v)`, which assigns `xs[i] = v` and may overwrite the previous value with either a larger or smaller value, or `('qry', i)`, which returns `max(xs[0:i+1])`. `xs` initially contains n zeros. Return query answers in order. Support arbitrary integer v. Target O(log n) per operation. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def point_update_prefix_max(n, ops):\n    size = 1\n    while size < n:\n        size <<= 1\n    seg = [0] * (2 * size)\n\n    def set_value(i, v):\n        p = size + i\n        seg[p] = v\n        p >>= 1\n        while p:\n            seg[p] = max(seg[p << 1], seg[p << 1 | 1])\n            p >>= 1\n\n    def prefix_max(i):\n        l = size\n        r = size + i + 1\n        ans = float('-inf')\n        while l < r:\n            if l & 1:\n                ans = max(ans, seg[l])\n                l += 1\n            if r & 1:\n                r -= 1\n                ans = max(ans, seg[r])\n            l >>= 1\n            r >>= 1\n        return ans\n\n    out = []\n    for op in ops:\n        if op[0] == 'set':\n            set_value(op[1], op[2])\n        else:\n            out.append(prefix_max(op[1]))\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert point_update_prefix_max(5, [('qry', 4)]) == [0]","assert point_update_prefix_max(5, [('set', 2, 7), ('qry', 4), ('qry', 1)]) == [7, 0]","assert point_update_prefix_max(5, [('set', 0, 3), ('set', 1, 5), ('qry', 1)]) == [5]","assert point_update_prefix_max(1, [('set', 0, 9), ('qry', 0)]) == [9]","assert point_update_prefix_max(3, [('set', 1, 5), ('set', 1, 3), ('qry', 1)]) == [3]","assert point_update_prefix_max(4, [('set', 0, 8), ('set', 3, 6), ('set', 0, 1), ('qry', 3), ('qry', 0)]) == [6, 1]","assert point_update_prefix_max(3, [('set', 0, -5), ('set', 1, -2), ('qry', 1), ('qry', 2)]) == [-2, 0]","assert point_update_prefix_max(3, [('set', 0, -5), ('set', 1, -2), ('set', 2, -9), ('qry', 2), ('qry', 1)]) == [-2, -2]"]},"tags":["optimize","bit"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.algorithmic_optimization","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"complexity_jump":"polynomial_to_polylog","data_structure_invention":"novel_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Given a directed graph as an adjacency dict `graph: dict[int, list[tuple[int, int]]]`, where every edge `(v, w)` has a non-negative integer weight `w`, implement `multi_source_shortest(graph: dict[int, list[tuple[int, int]]], sources: list[int], queries: list[int]) -> dict[int, int]`. Compute the minimum shortest-path distance from any node in `sources` to each node in `queries` using one multi-source Dijkstra run: initialize every source at distance 0 in the priority queue. Nodes absent from `graph` are treated as having no outgoing edges. Return `{query: distance}` and use `float('inf')` for unreachable queries. Your implementation should run in O((n+m) log n), aside from output construction. Output a Python function definition; imports inside the function are allowed.","ground_truth":"import heapq\ndef multi_source_shortest(graph, sources, queries):\n    dist = {s:0 for s in sources}\n    h = [(0, s) for s in sources]\n    heapq.heapify(h)\n    while h:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(h)\n        if d > dist[u]: continue\n        for v, w in graph.get(u, []):\n            nd = d + w\n            if v not in dist or nd < dist[v]:\n                dist[v] = nd\n                heapq.heappush(h, (nd, v))\n    return {q: dist.get(q, float('inf')) for q in queries}","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["g={1:[(2,10),(3,1)],3:[(2,1)],2:[]}; assert multi_source_shortest(g,[1],[2])=={2:2}","g={1:[(4,8)],2:[(3,1)],3:[(4,1)],4:[]}; assert multi_source_shortest(g,[1,2],[4,3])=={4:2,3:1}","g={1:[(2,10),(3,1)],3:[(2,1)],2:[(4,1)],4:[]}; assert multi_source_shortest(g,[1],[2,4])=={2:2,4:3}","g={1:[]}; assert multi_source_shortest(g,[1],[1,9])=={1:0,9:float('inf')}","g={1:[(2,2)],2:[(1,2)],3:[]}; assert multi_source_shortest(g,[1],[2,3])=={2:2,3:float('inf')}"]},"tags":["optimize","multi_source_dijkstra"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.component_authoring.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.component_authoring","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"tree_depth":"single_element","composition":"no_subcomponents"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component named `Greeting` that accepts a prop `name: string` and renders only one element: `<h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>`. The component may be exported or not. Output only the component and any necessary imports.","ground_truth":"export function Greeting({ name }: { name: string }) { return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>; }","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Greeting"],"component_contract":{"binding":"Greeting","props":{"name":"string"},"return_checks":{"required_patterns":["\\A\\s*\\(?\\s*<h1\\b[^>]*>\\s*Hello,\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*\\}!\\s*</h1>\\s*\\)?\\s*\\Z"],"jsx_tag_counts":{"h1":1}}}}},"tags":["component","flat"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.component_authoring.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.component_authoring","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"tree_depth":"single_element","composition":"no_subcomponents"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `Avatar` taking `src` and `alt` string props that renders a single <img> with those attributes. Output only the component.","ground_truth":"export function Avatar({src, alt}:{src:string; alt:string}){return <img src={src} alt={alt}/>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Avatar"],"props":{"src":"string","alt":"string"},"jsx_tag_counts":{"img":1},"required_patterns":["<img\\b(?=[^>]*\\bsrc\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*src\\s*\\})(?=[^>]*\\balt\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*alt\\s*\\})[^>]*/?>"],"component_contract":{"binding":"Avatar","return_checks":{"required_patterns":["<img\\b(?=[^>]*\\bsrc\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*src\\s*\\})(?=[^>]*\\balt\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*alt\\s*\\})[^>]*/?>"]}}}},"tags":["component","img"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.component_authoring.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.component_authoring","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"tree_depth":"few_children","composition":"no_subcomponents"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a React TSX component `Card` taking `title` (string) and `body` (string) props that renders a div containing an <h2> with the title and a <p> with the body. Output only the component.","ground_truth":"export function Card({title, body}:{title:string; body:string}){return <div><h2>{title}</h2><p>{body}</p></div>}","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"bindings":["Card"],"props":{"title":"string","body":"string"},"jsx_tag_counts":{"div":1,"h2":1,"p":1},"required_jsx_patterns":["<div\\b[^>]*>\\s*<h2\\b[^>]*>\\s*\\{\\s*title\\s*\\}\\s*</h2>\\s*<p\\b[^>]*>\\s*\\{\\s*body\\s*\\}\\s*</p>\\s*</div>"],"component_contract":{"binding":"Card","return_checks":{"required_patterns":["<div\\b[^>]*>\\s*<h2\\b[^>]*>\\s*\\{\\s*title\\s*\\}\\s*</h2>\\s*<p\\b[^>]*>\\s*\\{\\s*body\\s*\\}\\s*</p>\\s*</div>"]}}}},"tags":["component","flat"],"notes":""}
