{"probe_id":"code.import_resolution.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.import_resolution","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"symbol_count":"many_across_packages","default_vs_named":"mixed_default_named"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write all required import lines for the following TSX. Assume the automatic React JSX runtime (`jsx: react-jsx`), so no `React` default import is needed. Assume default imports are supported for `lodash` and `classnames`. Import `useState` from `react`; import lodash's default export as `_`; import classnames's default export as `cn`; and import the default export `Helper` plus named export `Tag` from `./Helper`. Output only import declarations.\n```tsx\nfunction Foo(){const [v, setV] = useState(''); const cls = cn('a', {b: !!v}); const xs = _.uniq([1,1,2]); return <Helper><Tag t='x'/></Helper>}\n```","ground_truth":"import { useState } from 'react';\nimport _ from 'lodash';\nimport cn from 'classnames';\nimport Helper, { Tag } from './Helper';","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"tsx","checks":{"imports":["useState","_","cn","Helper","Tag"],"import_sources":["react","lodash","classnames","./Helper"],"required_literal_patterns":["(?m)^[ \\t]*import[ \\t]+\\{(?=[^}\\n]*\\buseState\\b)[ \\t]*[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*(?:[ \\t]+as[ \\t]+[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*)?(?:[ \\t]*,[ \\t]*[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*(?:[ \\t]+as[ \\t]+[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*)?)*[ \\t]*\\}[ \\t]+from[ \\t]+(['\\\"])react\\1[ \\t]*;?[ \\t]*$","(?m)^[ \\t]*import[ \\t]+_[ \\t]+from[ \\t]+(['\\\"])lodash\\1[ \\t]*;?[ \\t]*$","(?m)^[ \\t]*import[ \\t]+cn[ \\t]+from[ \\t]+(['\\\"])classnames\\1[ \\t]*;?[ \\t]*$","(?m)^[ \\t]*import[ \\t]+Helper[ \\t]*,[ \\t]*\\{(?=[^}\\n]*\\bTag\\b)[ \\t]*[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*(?:[ \\t]+as[ \\t]+[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*)?(?:[ \\t]*,[ \\t]*[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*(?:[ \\t]+as[ \\t]+[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*)?)*[ \\t]*\\}[ \\t]+from[ \\t]+(['\\\"])\\./Helper\\1[ \\t]*;?[ \\t]*$"]}},"tags":["imports","default+named","local"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.import_resolution.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.import_resolution","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"symbol_count":"many_across_packages","default_vs_named":"mixed_default_named"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Assume this file is a module inside a Python package, and `schemas.py` is a sibling module in the same package. Write the minimal imports directly required by the following code. Output only import lines.\n```\nasync def fetch(url: str) -> Optional[User]:\n    async with AsyncClient() as c:\n        r = await c.get(url)\n        return User.model_validate(r.json())\n```","ground_truth":"from typing import Optional\nfrom httpx import AsyncClient\nfrom .schemas import User","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"python","checks":{"imports":["typing.Optional","httpx.AsyncClient",".schemas.User"],"only_imports":true}},"tags":["imports","python","async"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_in_prompt","arg_count":"1_arg","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the function `cube`. Tests:\n```\nassert cube(2) == 8\nassert cube(0) == 0\nassert cube(-3) == -27\n```\nOutput only the function definition.","ground_truth":"def cube(x):\n    return x*x*x","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"cube","tests":["assert cube(2) == 8","assert cube(0) == 0","assert cube(-3) == -27","assert cube(10) == 1000","assert cube(1) == 1"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","named"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_in_prompt","arg_count":"2_3_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `repeat_str(s, n)`. Tests:\n```\nassert repeat_str('ab', 3) == 'ababab'\nassert repeat_str('x', 0) == ''\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def repeat_str(s, n):\n    return s*n","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"repeat_str","tests":["assert repeat_str('ab', 3) == 'ababab'","assert repeat_str('x', 0) == ''","assert repeat_str('', 5) == ''","assert repeat_str('a', 1) == 'a'","assert repeat_str('ha', 2) == 'haha'"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","named"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_in_prompt","arg_count":"1_arg","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `to_upper(s)`. Tests:\n```\nassert to_upper('hi') == 'HI'\nassert to_upper('Hello World') == 'HELLO WORLD'\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def to_upper(s):\n    return s.upper()","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"to_upper","tests":["assert to_upper('hi') == 'HI'","assert to_upper('Hello World') == 'HELLO WORLD'","assert to_upper('') == ''","assert to_upper('123') == '123'","assert to_upper('aBcDe') == 'ABCDE'"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","named"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L1.004","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_in_prompt","arg_count":"2_3_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `nth_letter(s, n)` returning the n-th character (1-indexed) of s. Tests:\n```\nassert nth_letter('hello', 1) == 'h'\nassert nth_letter('hello', 5) == 'o'\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def nth_letter(s, n):\n    return s[n-1]","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"nth_letter","tests":["assert nth_letter('hello', 1) == 'h'","assert nth_letter('hello', 5) == 'o'","assert nth_letter('a', 1) == 'a'","assert nth_letter('abc', 2) == 'b'","assert nth_letter('xyz', 3) == 'z'"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","1_indexed"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L1.005","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_in_prompt","arg_count":"1_arg","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `length_squared(xs)`. Tests:\n```\nassert length_squared([]) == 0\nassert length_squared([1,2,3]) == 9\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def length_squared(xs):\n    return len(xs)**2","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"length_squared","tests":["assert length_squared([]) == 0","assert length_squared([1,2,3]) == 9","assert length_squared([0]) == 1","assert length_squared([1]*10) == 100","assert length_squared([1,2]) == 4"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L2.001","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"1_arg","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the function shown only by the tests below. For a non-negative integer n, return whether n is one less than twice the integer formed by reversing its decimal digits. Tests:\n```\nassert check(70) == False\nassert check(73) == True\nassert check(31) == False\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def check(n):\n    rev = int(str(n)[::-1])\n    return n == 2*rev - 1","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert check(70) == False","assert check(73) == True","assert check(31) == False","assert check(1) == True","assert check(99) == False"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","name_in_tests"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L2.002","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"2_3_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the function shown by the prose and tests below. Count overlapping occurrences of substring sub in string s. Return 0 when sub is empty. Tests:\n```\nassert occur('ababab', 'ab') == 3\nassert occur('abc', 'd') == 0\nassert occur('aaaa', 'aa') == 3\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def occur(s, sub):\n    if not sub:\n        return 0\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(len(s) - len(sub) + 1):\n        if s[i:i + len(sub)] == sub:\n            count += 1\n    return count","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"occur","tests":["assert occur('ababab', 'ab') == 3","assert occur('abc', 'd') == 0","assert occur('aaaa', 'aa') == 3","assert occur('', 'x') == 0","assert occur('hello', 'l') == 2","assert occur('abc', '') == 0"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L2.003","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"1_arg","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the function under test, shown only by tests; prose: 'check whether n is a Woodall number' (n = m * 2^m - 1 for some positive integer m). Tests:\n```\nassert is_woodall(7) == True\nassert is_woodall(383) == True\nassert is_woodall(64) == False\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def is_woodall(n):\n    if n < 1: return False\n    m = 1\n    while True:\n        v = m * (1 << m) - 1\n        if v == n: return True\n        if v > n: return False\n        m += 1","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert is_woodall(-1) is False","assert is_woodall(0) is False","assert is_woodall(1) is True","assert is_woodall(2) is False","assert is_woodall(7) is True","assert is_woodall(11) is False","assert is_woodall(23) is True","assert is_woodall(63) is True","assert is_woodall(64) is False","assert is_woodall(159) is True","assert is_woodall(160) is False","assert is_woodall(383) is True","assert is_woodall(384) is False","assert is_woodall(1023) is False"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","math"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L2.004","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"2_3_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the Python function under test (name visible only in tests); prose: 'returns the n-th element of the Fibonacci sequence (0-indexed)'. Tests:\n```\nassert fib(0) == 0\nassert fib(1) == 1\nassert fib(7) == 13\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def fib(n):\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(n): a, b = b, a+b\n    return a","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"fib","tests":["assert fib(0) == 0","assert fib(1) == 1","assert fib(2) == 1","assert fib(3) == 2","assert fib(7) == 13","assert fib(8) == 21","assert fib(10) == 55","assert fib(20) == 6765"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L2.005","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"1_arg","arg_order_ambiguity":"unambiguous"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the Python function inferred from the following requirement and tests. The function signature is `def squash(s: str) -> str`. Remove duplicate consecutive characters, retaining one character from each consecutive run. Output only the function.\n```\nassert squash('aabbcc') == 'abc'\nassert squash('aaabbb') == 'ab'\nassert squash('') == ''\n```","ground_truth":"def squash(s):\n    out = ''\n    for c in s:\n        if not out or out[-1] != c: out += c\n    return out","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"squash","tests":["assert squash('aabbcc') == 'abc'","assert squash('aaabbb') == 'ab'","assert squash('') == ''","assert squash('a') == 'a'","assert squash('ababab') == 'ababab'","assert squash('aabcccaa') == 'abca'","assert squash('!!  --') == '! -'"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L3.001","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"2_3_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"swappable_pair"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the function shown only by tests. It computes a base raised to an exponent. The argument order must be inferred from the test values: one argument is the integer base and the other is the non-negative integer exponent. For exponent 0, return 1. Tests:\n```\nassert powp(2, 10) == 1024\nassert powp(3, 4) == 81\nassert powp(5, 0) == 1\n```\nOutput only the function.","ground_truth":"def powp(base, exp):\n    r = 1\n    for _ in range(exp): r *= base\n    return r","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"powp","tests":["assert powp(2, 10) == 1024","assert powp(3, 4) == 81","assert powp(5, 0) == 1","assert powp(7, 1) == 7","assert powp(10, 3) == 1000","assert powp(6, 4) == 1296"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","arg_order"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L3.002","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"2_3_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"swappable_pair"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the function shown by the tests. It computes the area of a triangle as 1/2 * base * height. Output only the function. Any valid two-positional-argument implementation that returns the correct area is acceptable.\n```\nassert tri(10, 4) == 20.0\nassert tri(5, 6) == 15.0\nassert tri(7, 2) == 7.0\n```","ground_truth":"def tri(base, height):\n    return 0.5 * base * height","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert tri(10, 4) == 20.0","assert tri(5, 6) == 15.0","assert tri(7, 2) == 7.0","assert tri(1, 1) == 0.5","assert tri(0, 100) == 0.0"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","arg_order"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"4_plus_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"many_orderings"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement `epoch(year, month, day, hour)`, returning the integer number of seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC for a valid Gregorian date and hour. Inputs are within Python `datetime`'s supported range. Use `datetime` with an explicit UTC timezone. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def epoch(year, month, day, hour):\n    from datetime import datetime, timezone\n    return int(datetime(year, month, day, hour, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp())","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert epoch(1970, 1, 1, 0) == 0","assert epoch(1970, 1, 1, 1) == 3600","assert epoch(1970, 2, 1, 0) == 31 * 86400","assert epoch(1970, 4, 1, 0) == 90 * 86400","assert epoch(1971, 1, 1, 0) == 31536000","assert epoch(1972, 1, 1, 0) == 63072000","assert epoch(1972, 2, 29, 0) == 68169600","assert epoch(2000, 3, 1, 0) == 951868800","assert epoch(1999, 12, 31, 23) == 946681200"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","many_args"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"4_plus_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"many_orderings"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the function shown only by tests; prose: 'Pack four 8-bit channel intensities into a 32-bit ARGB integer (alpha in the most significant byte, then red, green, blue). Each input is an integer in the range 0..255.' Tests:\n```\nassert argb(255, 255, 0, 0) == 0xFFFF0000\nassert argb(255, 0, 255, 0) == 0xFF00FF00\nassert argb(255, 0, 0, 255) == 0xFF0000FF\nassert argb(0, 0, 0, 0) == 0\n```\nDeduce argument order. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def argb(a, r, g, b):\n    return (a << 24) | (r << 16) | (g << 8) | b","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"argb","tests":["assert argb(255, 255, 0, 0) == 0xFFFF0000","assert argb(255, 0, 255, 0) == 0xFF00FF00","assert argb(255, 0, 0, 255) == 0xFF0000FF","assert argb(0, 0, 0, 0) == 0","assert argb(128, 128, 128, 128) == 0x80808080","assert argb(1, 2, 4, 8) == 0x01020408","assert argb(17, 34, 51, 68) == 0x11223344"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","bitfield"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.signature_from_tests.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.signature_from_tests","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"name_visibility":"named_only_in_tests","arg_count":"4_plus_args","arg_order_ambiguity":"many_orderings"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Implement the Python function shown only by tests; prose: 'linear interpolate from x in [a, b] to [c, d]'. Tests:\n```\nassert lerp(0.5, 0, 1, 10, 20) == 15.0\nassert lerp(0, 0, 10, 100, 200) == 100.0\nassert lerp(10, 0, 10, 0, 1) == 1.0\n```\nDeduce arg order. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def lerp(x, a, b, c, d):\n    return c + (x - a) * (d - c) / (b - a)","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"lerp","tests":["assert lerp(0.5, 0, 1, 10, 20) == 15.0","assert lerp(0, 0, 10, 100, 200) == 100.0","assert lerp(10, 0, 10, 0, 1) == 1.0","assert lerp(5, 0, 10, 0, 100) == 50.0","assert lerp(2, 0, 4, -1, 1) == 0.0","assert abs(lerp(3, 2, 6, -5, 7) - (-2.0)) < 1e-12","assert abs(lerp(-1.25, -2, 2, 4, 12) - 5.5) < 1e-12","assert abs(lerp(7, 4, 10, 30, -6) - 12.0) < 1e-12"]},"tags":["sig_from_tests","many_args"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_branches":"two","predicate_complexity":"simple_comparison"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `sign(x: int) -> str` returning 'positive' if x>0 else 'non-positive'. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def sign(x: int) -> str:\n    if x > 0:\n        return 'positive'\n    return 'non-positive'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"sign","tests":["assert sign(1) == 'positive'","assert sign(0) == 'non-positive'","assert sign(-5) == 'non-positive'","assert sign(100) == 'positive'","assert sign(-1) == 'non-positive'","assert sign(42) == 'positive'","assert sign(-999) == 'non-positive'"]},"tags":["cond","two_branch"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L1.002","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_branches":"two","predicate_complexity":"simple_comparison"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `is_adult(age: int) -> bool` returning True iff age>=18. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def is_adult(age: int) -> bool:\n    return age >= 18","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"is_adult","tests":["assert is_adult(17) is False","assert is_adult(18) is True","assert is_adult(0) is False","assert is_adult(99) is True","assert is_adult(-1) is False"]},"tags":["cond","two_branch"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L1.003","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_branches":"two","predicate_complexity":"simple_comparison"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `parity(n: int) -> str` returning 'even' or 'odd'. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def parity(n):\n    return 'even' if n % 2 == 0 else 'odd'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"parity","tests":["assert parity(0) == 'even'","assert parity(1) == 'odd'","assert parity(-2) == 'even'","assert parity(7) == 'odd'","assert parity(100) == 'even'"]},"tags":["cond","two_branch"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L1.004","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_branches":"two","predicate_complexity":"simple_comparison"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `bigger(a: int, b: int) -> int` returning whichever is larger; if equal return either. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def bigger(a, b):\n    if a >= b: return a\n    return b","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"bigger","tests":["assert bigger(3, 5) == 5","assert bigger(5, 3) == 5","assert bigger(2, 2) == 2","assert bigger(-1, -3) == -1","assert bigger(0, 0) == 0"]},"tags":["cond","two_branch"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L1.005","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_branches":"two","predicate_complexity":"simple_comparison"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `passing(score: int) -> str` returning 'pass' if score>=60 else 'fail'. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def passing(score):\n    return 'pass' if score >= 60 else 'fail'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"passing","tests":["assert passing(60) == 'pass'","assert passing(59) == 'fail'","assert passing(0) == 'fail'","assert passing(100) == 'pass'","assert passing(75) == 'pass'"]},"tags":["cond","two_branch"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L2.001","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"simple_comparison"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `grade(score: int) -> str` returning 'A' if score>=90, 'B' if 80<=score<90, 'C' if 70<=score<80, 'F' otherwise. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def grade(score):\n    if score >= 90: return 'A'\n    elif score >= 80: return 'B'\n    elif score >= 70: return 'C'\n    return 'F'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"grade","tests":["assert grade(95) == 'A'","assert grade(90) == 'A'","assert grade(89) == 'B'","assert grade(80) == 'B'","assert grade(79) == 'C'","assert grade(70) == 'C'","assert grade(69) == 'F'"]},"tags":["cond","four_branch"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L2.002","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_branches":"three","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `triangle_kind(a: int, b: int, c: int) -> int` returning -1 if the side lengths cannot form a triangle (a+b<=c or a+c<=b or b+c<=a), 0 if scalene, 1 if isosceles, 2 if equilateral. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def triangle_kind(a, b, c):\n    if a+b <= c or a+c <= b or b+c <= a: return -1\n    if a == b == c: return 2\n    if a == b or b == c or a == c: return 1\n    return 0","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert triangle_kind(1, 1, 3) == -1","assert triangle_kind(1, 3, 1) == -1","assert triangle_kind(3, 1, 1) == -1","assert triangle_kind(3, 3, 3) == 2","assert triangle_kind(3, 3, 5) == 1","assert triangle_kind(3, 5, 3) == 1","assert triangle_kind(5, 3, 3) == 1","assert triangle_kind(3, 4, 5) == 0","assert triangle_kind(5, 12, 13) == 0"]},"tags":["cond","compound_predicate"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L2.003","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_branches":"three","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `discount(price: float, is_member: bool, qty: int) -> float`. Rules: if is_member AND qty>=5 → 30% off; else if is_member OR qty>=10 → 15% off; else no discount. Returns the final price. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def discount(price: float, is_member: bool, qty: int) -> float:\n    if is_member and qty >= 5:\n        return price * 0.7\n    if is_member or qty >= 10:\n        return price * 0.85\n    return price","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"discount","tests":["assert discount(100, True, 5) == 70.0","assert discount(100, True, 10) == 70.0","assert discount(100, True, 4) == 85.0","assert discount(100, False, 10) == 85.0","assert discount(100, False, 9) == 100.0","assert discount(100, False, 5) == 100.0","assert discount(0, True, 100) == 0.0"]},"tags":["cond","compound"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L2.004","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"type_or_membership_test"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `dispatch(x) -> str` using Python `isinstance` semantics. Return 'int' if `x` is an instance of `int` but not `bool`, 'bool' if it is an instance of `bool`, 'str' if it is an instance of `str`, 'list' if it is an instance of `list`, and 'other' otherwise. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def dispatch(x):\n    if isinstance(x, bool): return 'bool'\n    if isinstance(x, int): return 'int'\n    if isinstance(x, str): return 'str'\n    if isinstance(x, list): return 'list'\n    return 'other'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"dispatch","tests":["assert dispatch(5) == 'int'","assert dispatch(True) == 'bool'","assert dispatch(False) == 'bool'","assert dispatch('hi') == 'str'","assert dispatch([1, 2]) == 'list'","assert dispatch(3.14) == 'other'","assert dispatch(None) == 'other'","assert dispatch((1, 2)) == 'other'","class MyInt(int): pass\nassert dispatch(MyInt(7)) == 'int'","class MyStr(str): pass\nassert dispatch(MyStr('x')) == 'str'","class MyList(list): pass\nassert dispatch(MyList([1])) == 'list'","class Other: pass\nassert dispatch(Other()) == 'other'"]},"tags":["cond","types"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L2.005","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function `season(month: int) -> str` returning 'winter' for 12, 1, 2; 'spring' for 3, 4, 5; 'summer' for 6, 7, 8; and 'autumn' for 9, 10, 11. Months outside 1..12 return 'invalid'. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def season(month: int) -> str:\n    if not 1 <= month <= 12:\n        return 'invalid'\n    if month in (12, 1, 2):\n        return 'winter'\n    if month in (3, 4, 5):\n        return 'spring'\n    if month in (6, 7, 8):\n        return 'summer'\n    return 'autumn'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert season(1) == 'winter'","assert season(2) == 'winter'","assert season(3) == 'spring'","assert season(4) == 'spring'","assert season(5) == 'spring'","assert season(6) == 'summer'","assert season(7) == 'summer'","assert season(8) == 'summer'","assert season(9) == 'autumn'","assert season(10) == 'autumn'","assert season(11) == 'autumn'","assert season(12) == 'winter'","assert season(-1) == 'invalid'","assert season(0) == 'invalid'","assert season(13) == 'invalid'"]},"tags":["cond","membership"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L3.001","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `route(method: str, path: str, auth: bool) -> int` returning HTTP status: 401 if auth is False AND path != '/login'; 405 if method not in {'GET','POST'}; 404 if method == 'GET' and path not in {'/', '/health', '/login'}; 200 otherwise. Branches must short-circuit in this order. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def route(method, path, auth):\n    if not auth and path != '/login': return 401\n    if method not in {'GET','POST'}: return 405\n    if method == 'GET' and path not in {'/', '/health', '/login'}: return 404\n    return 200","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","language":"python","entry_point":"route","tests":["assert route('GET','/login', False) == 200","assert route('GET','/', False) == 401","assert route('PUT','/', False) == 401","assert route('GET','/missing', False) == 401","assert route('PUT','/', True) == 405","assert route('DELETE','/login', False) == 405","assert route('GET','/missing', True) == 404","assert route('POST','/anything', True) == 200"]},"tags":["cond","short_circuit"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L3.002","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `tax(income: float, country: str, married: bool) -> float`. Return a flat tax amount: first select exactly one rate using the rules below, then return `income * rate` for the entire income. Do not use marginal or progressive taxation. For `country == 'US'`, use 10% when income is less than 10000, 22% when income is at least 10000 and less than 50000, and 35% otherwise; if married is true, double both US thresholds to 20000 and 100000. For `country == 'UK'`, use 20% when income is less than 50000, otherwise 40%. For every other country, use 25%. `married` affects only US thresholds. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def tax(income: float, country: str, married: bool) -> float:\n    if country == 'US':\n        first_threshold = 20000 if married else 10000\n        second_threshold = 100000 if married else 50000\n        if income < first_threshold:\n            return income * 0.10\n        if income < second_threshold:\n            return income * 0.22\n        return income * 0.35\n    if country == 'UK':\n        return income * (0.20 if income < 50000 else 0.40)\n    return income * 0.25","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert abs(tax(5000, 'US', False) - 500.0) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(10000, 'US', False) - 2200.0) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(49999, 'US', False) - 10999.78) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(50000, 'US', False) - 17500.0) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(20000, 'US', True) - 4400.0) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(99999, 'US', True) - 21999.78) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(100000, 'US', True) - 35000.0) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(49999, 'UK', False) - 9999.8) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(50000, 'UK', True) - 20000.0) < 1e-6","assert abs(tax(1000, 'DE', False) - 250.0) < 1e-6"]},"tags":["cond","nested"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L3.003","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write a Python function `validate_password(s: str) -> str`. Return `'ok'` only if `len(s) >= 8` and at least one character satisfies each of `str.islower()`, `str.isupper()`, and `str.isdigit()`. Otherwise return the FIRST failing reason, in this exact order: `'too_short'`, `'no_lower'`, `'no_upper'`, `'no_digit'`. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def validate_password(s: str) -> str:\n    if len(s) < 8:\n        return 'too_short'\n    if not any(c.islower() for c in s):\n        return 'no_lower'\n    if not any(c.isupper() for c in s):\n        return 'no_upper'\n    if not any(c.isdigit() for c in s):\n        return 'no_digit'\n    return 'ok'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert validate_password('abc') == 'too_short'","assert validate_password('Abc1234') == 'too_short'","assert validate_password('ABCD1234') == 'no_lower'","assert validate_password('abcd1234') == 'no_upper'","assert validate_password('Abcdefgh') == 'no_digit'","assert validate_password('12345678') == 'no_lower'","assert validate_password('abcdefgh') == 'no_upper'","assert validate_password('ABCDEFGH') == 'no_lower'","assert validate_password('Abcd1234') == 'ok'"]},"tags":["cond","ordered_checks"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L3.004","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `pick_command(input_present: bool, has_lock: bool, force: bool, dry_run: bool) -> str`. Return the command word according to this priority order: if not input_present return 'noop'; otherwise, if has_lock and not force return 'wait'; otherwise, if force and dry_run return 'dry-force'; otherwise, if force return 'force-run'; otherwise, if dry_run return 'dry-run'; otherwise return 'run'. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def pick_command(input_present: bool, has_lock: bool, force: bool, dry_run: bool) -> str:\n    if not input_present:\n        return 'noop'\n    if has_lock and not force:\n        return 'wait'\n    if force and dry_run:\n        return 'dry-force'\n    if force:\n        return 'force-run'\n    if dry_run:\n        return 'dry-run'\n    return 'run'","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["from itertools import product","for input_present, has_lock, force, dry_run in product([False, True], repeat=4):","    expected = ('noop' if not input_present else 'wait' if has_lock and not force else 'dry-force' if force and dry_run else 'force-run' if force else 'dry-run' if dry_run else 'run')","    assert pick_command(input_present, has_lock, force, dry_run) == expected"]},"tags":["cond","nested","priority"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.conditional_logic.L3.005","canonical_id":"code.conditional_logic","category":"code","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_branches":"four_plus","predicate_complexity":"compound_boolean"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Write `route_status(role: str, owns: bool, path_pub: bool, deleted: bool) -> int`. Apply conditions in this priority order: return 410 if `deleted`; otherwise return 401 if `role == 'guest' and not path_pub`; otherwise return 200 if `role == 'guest' and path_pub` (`owns` is irrelevant); otherwise return 403 if `role == 'member' and not (owns or path_pub)`; otherwise return 200. Output only the function.","ground_truth":"def route_status(role, owns, path_pub, deleted):\n    if deleted:\n        return 410\n    if role == 'guest' and not path_pub:\n        return 401\n    if role == 'guest' and path_pub:\n        return 200\n    if role == 'member' and not (owns or path_pub):\n        return 403\n    return 200","scorer":{"type":"code_unittest","tests":["assert route_status('guest', False, False, True) == 410","assert route_status('member', False, False, True) == 410","assert route_status('guest', False, False, False) == 401","assert route_status('guest', True, False, False) == 401","assert route_status('guest', False, True, False) == 200","assert route_status('guest', True, True, False) == 200","assert route_status('member', False, False, False) == 403","assert route_status('member', True, False, False) == 200","assert route_status('member', False, True, False) == 200","assert route_status('admin', False, False, False) == 200"]},"tags":["cond","priority","nested"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"code.api_idiomatic_use.L1.001","canonical_id":"code.api_idiomatic_use","category":"code","level":"L1","axis_values":{"api_obscurity":"mainstream","composition_depth":"single_call","version_sensitivity":"stable"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Assume `import pandas as pd` has already been run. Write one Python assignment statement that reads the CSV file at path `data.csv` into a DataFrame named `df` using pandas. Output only that assignment line.","ground_truth":"df = pd.read_csv('data.csv')","scorer":{"type":"code_parse_ast","parser":"python","checks":{"assignment_call":{"target":"df","call":"pd.read_csv","args":["data.csv"]}}},"tags":["api","pandas"],"notes":""}
